-- English syllabus gap-fill and hard MCQs, written for Markhor Prep.
-- Audit of the PMDC MDCAT 2025 English learning outcomes (1.1 to 3.4) and the NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmarks (1 to 6)
-- against ENG-0001..ENG-0473 found topics with no or very few questions: word parts (prefixes, suffixes, roots), word
-- relationships, imagery and the senses, satire/parody, dash/brackets/abbreviations/ellipsis, capital letters, comma splices
-- and run-ons, dangling modifiers, redundancy, function-word errors (fewer/less, number/amount, lie/lay), the four kinds of
-- sentences, participial/absolute/appositive phrases, perfect and passive infinitives, gerund functions, wishes, subjunctive,
-- mixed conditionals, impersonal and causative passives, emphasis/summary/concession transitions, and more reading passages.
-- ENG-0474 to ENG-0770: 96 gap-fill questions plus 201 hard questions across all 15 chapters.
-- Not taken from any book. Each question's source names the learning outcome it tests. Not in the free set.
--
-- Banks: like migrations 0021 and 0030, English goes in MDCAT, AKU, and NUMS (NUMS leaves out the purely literary items:
-- figures of speech, imagery, irony, satire, parody, tone). INSERT OR IGNORE keeps the file safe to run twice; if the site
-- owner's admin account doesn't exist (a fresh local database) nothing is inserted. Batches of 20 rows stay under D1's statement size limit.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0474', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Word parts: prefixes, suffixes and roots', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.3', 'In the word "hypoglycaemia", the prefix "hypo-" means:', '["above or more than normal", "without", "below or less than normal", "around"]', 2, '''Hypo-'' means under or below normal, so hypoglycaemia is a blood sugar level below normal.', '{"0": "That is ''hyper-'', as in hyperglycaemia.", "1": "''Without'' is the prefix ''a-'' or ''an-'', as in anaemia.", "3": "''Around'' is ''peri-'', as in pericardium."}'),
  ('ENG-0475', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Word parts: prefixes, suffixes and roots', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.3', 'Knowing that "brady-" means slow and "cardia" refers to the heart, "bradycardia" most likely means:', '["an unusually slow heart rate", "an unusually fast heart rate", "an irregular heartbeat", "an enlarged heart"]', 0, '''Brady-'' (slow) + ''cardia'' (heart) gives a slow heart rate.', '{"1": "A fast heart rate is tachycardia (''tachy-'' means fast).", "2": "An irregular beat is an arrhythmia.", "3": "An enlarged heart is cardiomegaly (''-megaly'' means enlargement)."}'),
  ('ENG-0476', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Word parts: prefixes, suffixes and roots', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.3', 'The suffix "-itis", as in "tonsillitis" and "appendicitis", means:', '["surgical removal", "the study of", "a tumour", "inflammation"]', 3, '''-itis'' always signals inflammation of the named part.', '{"0": "Removal is ''-ectomy'', as in appendectomy.", "1": "''The study of'' is ''-logy''.", "2": "A tumour is ''-oma'', as in carcinoma."}'),
  ('ENG-0477', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Word parts: prefixes, suffixes and roots', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.3', 'In which word does the prefix mean "not"?', '["illuminate", "illegible", "immerse", "inflammable"]', 1, '''Il-'' in illegible is the negative prefix: not legible (cannot be read).', '{"0": "Here ''il-'' is a form of ''in-'' meaning upon or into: to throw light on, not ''not luminate''.", "2": "''Im-'' here means into: to dip into a liquid.", "3": "''In-'' here means into or intensifies; inflammable means easily set on fire, the same as flammable."}'),
  ('ENG-0478', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Word parts: prefixes, suffixes and roots', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.3', '"Bilateral" weakness affects:', '["one side of the body only", "both sides of the body", "many parts of the body", "neither side of the body"]', 1, '''Bi-'' means two and ''lateral'' means side, so bilateral means on both sides.', '{"0": "That is ''unilateral'' (''uni-'' means one).", "2": "''Many'' would be ''multi-''.", "3": "Nothing in the word suggests ''neither''."}'),
  ('ENG-0479', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Word parts: prefixes, suffixes and roots', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.3', 'The root "chron", found in "chronic" and "chronology", is connected with:', '["colour", "light", "heat", "time"]', 3, '''Chron'' comes from the Greek for time: a chronic illness lasts a long time; chronology is the order of events in time.', '{"0": "Colour is the root ''chrom'', as in chromosome.", "1": "Light is ''photo''.", "2": "Heat is ''therm''."}'),
  ('ENG-0480', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Word parts: prefixes, suffixes and roots', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.3', 'The word "antiseptic" contains a prefix that means:', '["against", "before", "after", "within"]', 0, '''Anti-'' means against: an antiseptic works against infection (sepsis).', '{"1": "''Before'' is ''ante-'' (as in antenatal), a common confusion with ''anti-''.", "2": "''After'' is ''post-''.", "3": "''Within'' is ''intra-'' or ''endo-''."}'),
  ('ENG-0481', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Word parts: prefixes, suffixes and roots', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.3', 'Which suffix turns the verb "treat" into an adjective meaning "able to be treated"?', '["-ment", "-er", "-able", "-ise"]', 2, '''-able'' means capable of being: treatable = able to be treated.', '{"0": "''-ment'' makes a noun (treatment).", "1": "''-er'' makes a noun for a person or thing that does the action.", "3": "''-ise'' makes verbs, and ''treatise'' is a noun meaning a written study."}'),
  ('ENG-0482', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Word parts: prefixes, suffixes and roots', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.3', '"Insomnia" is best defined as:', '["sleeping too much", "walking while asleep", "an inability to sleep", "a fear of the dark"]', 2, '''In-'' (not) + ''somn'' (sleep): the condition of not being able to sleep.', '{"0": "Excessive sleep is hypersomnia.", "1": "Sleepwalking is somnambulism.", "3": "That is nyctophobia; nothing in ''insomnia'' refers to fear."}'),
  ('ENG-0483', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Word parts: prefixes, suffixes and roots', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.3', 'In which word is "bi-" NOT a prefix meaning "two"?', '["bias", "bisect", "binocular", "biennial"]', 0, 'In ''bias'' the letters ''bi'' are simply the start of the word, not a prefix; the word does not mean ''two'' of anything.', '{"1": "Bisect means to cut into two parts.", "2": "Binocular means using both (two) eyes.", "3": "Biennial means happening every two years."}'),
  ('ENG-0484', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "insidious" most nearly means:
"The disease was so insidious that it went unnoticed for years while it damaged the kidneys."', '["sudden and violent", "easily cured", "spreading gradually and harmfully without being noticed", "openly visible"]', 2, '''Insidious'' describes harm that creeps in unseen; the sentence says it went unnoticed for years while causing damage.', '{"0": "A sudden, violent illness would not go unnoticed for years.", "1": "Nothing suggests a cure; it caused damage.", "3": "It went unnoticed, which is the opposite."}'),
  ('ENG-0485', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "platitudes" most nearly means:
"The minister''s speech was full of platitudes, offering nothing the audience had not heard a hundred times."', '["remarks so often repeated that they have lost their force", "bold new proposals", "precise statistics", "personal insults"]', 0, 'A platitude is a trite, overused remark; the clue is ''nothing the audience had not heard a hundred times''.', '{"1": "New proposals would be things the audience had not heard.", "2": "Statistics are not described by the clue.", "3": "Nothing suggests the speech was insulting."}'),
  ('ENG-0486', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "corroborated" most nearly means:
"Her account of the accident was corroborated by two independent witnesses, so the police accepted it."', '["contradicted", "exaggerated", "recorded", "confirmed"]', 3, 'To corroborate is to support with further evidence; the police accepted the account because witnesses confirmed it.', '{"0": "If witnesses contradicted her, the police would not have accepted it.", "1": "Witnesses do not exaggerate someone else''s account.", "2": "Recording an account would not make it believable."}'),
  ('ENG-0487', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "equanimity" most nearly means:
"The surgeon''s equanimity during the crisis steadied the whole team."', '["technical skill", "calmness under pressure", "anger", "haste"]', 1, 'Equanimity is mental calmness; only a calm leader would ''steady'' a team in a crisis.', '{"0": "Skill alone would not explain the steadying effect on emotions.", "2": "Anger would unsettle the team.", "3": "Hurrying would not steady anyone."}'),
  ('ENG-0488', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "moribund" most nearly means:
"Once thriving, the town is now moribund: its factories are shut and its young people have left."', '["overcrowded", "being rebuilt", "peaceful and quiet", "close to dying out"]', 3, 'Moribund means in terminal decline; closed factories and departing youth describe a dying town.', '{"0": "The young people have left, so it is emptying, not crowded.", "1": "Nothing suggests rebuilding.", "2": "Quietness is a side-effect; the word means dying."}'),
  ('ENG-0489', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "mitigate" most nearly means:
"He tried to mitigate the damage by apologising at once."', '["make less severe", "cause", "measure", "hide"]', 0, 'An immediate apology is meant to lessen the damage, which is what mitigate means.', '{"1": "He was not trying to cause damage.", "2": "An apology does not measure anything.", "3": "An apology admits the damage rather than hiding it."}'),
  ('ENG-0490', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "cursory" most nearly means:
"A cursory glance at the X-ray missed the hairline fracture that a specialist later found."', '["careful", "expert", "hasty and superficial", "repeated"]', 2, 'The glance missed a fine fracture that careful examination later found, so it was quick and shallow.', '{"0": "A careful look would have found the fracture.", "1": "The specialist was the expert who found it.", "3": "Nothing suggests it was repeated."}'),
  ('ENG-0491', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "vindicated" most nearly means:
"The new evidence vindicated the scientist whose theory had been ridiculed for decades."', '["embarrassed further", "replaced", "proved right", "punished"]', 2, 'To vindicate is to show someone was right after doubt; the theory had been ridiculed.', '{"0": "Evidence that vindicates removes embarrassment.", "1": "Nothing suggests someone took her place.", "3": "The evidence supports, not punishes."}'),
  ('ENG-0492', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "laconic" most nearly means:
"His laconic reply, a single "No", ended the discussion."', '["using very few words", "rude and insulting", "furious", "long-winded"]', 0, 'A one-word reply is laconic, meaning brief to the point of seeming abrupt.', '{"1": "\"No\" is short, not necessarily insulting.", "2": "Nothing shows anger.", "3": "One word is the opposite of long-winded."}'),
  ('ENG-0493', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "efficacy" most nearly means:
"The drug''s efficacy has been shown in three large trials, so it is now widely prescribed."', '["harmful side effects", "low price", "popularity", "ability to produce the intended result"]', 3, 'Trials are run to show that a drug works; being widely prescribed follows from proven efficacy.', '{"0": "Proven side effects would not lead to wider use.", "1": "Trials do not show price.", "2": "Popularity follows; it is not what trials prove."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0494', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "precipitate" most nearly means:
"His precipitate decision to resign, made within minutes of the meeting, surprised everyone."', '["a solid that settles out of a solution", "made too hastily", "carefully considered", "long delayed"]', 1, '''Precipitate'' as an adjective means rash or hasty; ''within minutes'' is the clue. Its chemistry meaning (a solid formed in a solution) does not fit here.', '{"0": "That is the noun used in chemistry, not the adjective here.", "2": "A decision made within minutes was not carefully considered.", "3": "The decision was quick, not delayed."}'),
  ('ENG-0495', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "abstruse" most nearly means:
"The lecture was so abstruse that even the senior students struggled to follow it."', '["very brief", "loud", "entertaining", "difficult to understand"]', 3, 'Abstruse means obscure and hard to understand; even senior students could not follow.', '{"0": "A short lecture would not be hard to follow for that reason.", "1": "Loudness does not make a lecture hard to understand.", "2": "An entertaining lecture is usually easy to follow."}'),
  ('ENG-0496', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "equivocal" most nearly means:
"Her answer was equivocal; she would neither confirm nor deny that she had seen the accused."', '["clear and firm", "completely false", "deliberately unclear", "very emotional"]', 2, 'An equivocal statement can be taken more than one way; neither confirming nor denying is the clue.', '{"0": "She refused to commit either way.", "1": "Nothing shows she lied.", "3": "Emotion is not suggested."}'),
  ('ENG-0497', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "apathy" most nearly means:
"The new rules were met with apathy; hardly anyone even bothered to read them."', '["anger", "enthusiasm", "lack of interest", "confusion"]', 2, '''Hardly anyone bothered to read them'' shows people did not care.', '{"0": "Angry people would at least read the rules.", "1": "Enthusiasm is the opposite.", "3": "Confusion would require reading them first."}'),
  ('ENG-0498', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Word relationships (analogies)', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.3', 'Choose the word that completes the analogy.
Doctor : Hospital :: Teacher : ______', '["Student", "Lesson", "Book", "School"]', 3, 'A doctor works in a hospital; a teacher works in a school (worker : workplace).', '{"0": "A student is taught by a teacher, not the place where a teacher works.", "1": "A lesson is what a teacher gives, not a workplace.", "2": "A book is a tool, not a workplace."}'),
  ('ENG-0499', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Word relationships (analogies)', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.3', 'Choose the word that completes the analogy.
Scalpel : Surgeon :: Brush : ______', '["Canvas", "Painter", "Colour", "Gallery"]', 1, 'A scalpel is the tool a surgeon uses; a brush is the tool a painter uses (tool : user).', '{"0": "The canvas is what is painted on, not the user of the brush.", "2": "Colour is the material, not the user.", "3": "A gallery is where paintings are shown."}'),
  ('ENG-0500', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Word relationships (analogies)', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.3', 'Choose the word that completes the analogy.
Pulmonary : Lungs :: Renal : ______', '["Heart", "Kidneys", "Liver", "Brain"]', 1, '''Pulmonary'' is the adjective for the lungs; ''renal'' is the adjective for the kidneys.', '{"0": "The adjective for the heart is ''cardiac''.", "2": "The adjective for the liver is ''hepatic''.", "3": "The adjective for the brain is ''cerebral''."}'),
  ('ENG-0501', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Word relationships (analogies)', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.3', 'Choose the word that completes the analogy.
Drought : Rain :: Famine : ______', '["Hunger", "Crop", "Farmer", "Food"]', 3, 'A drought is a severe lack of rain; a famine is a severe lack of food.', '{"0": "Hunger is a result of famine, not the thing that is lacking.", "1": "A crop failure may cause famine, but famine is the lack of food itself.", "2": "A farmer is a person, not the missing resource."}'),
  ('ENG-0502', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Word relationships (analogies)', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.3', 'Choose the word that completes the analogy.
Verbose : Concise :: Timid : ______', '["Bold", "Shy", "Fearful", "Quiet"]', 0, 'Verbose (wordy) is the opposite of concise; timid is the opposite of bold.', '{"1": "Shy is a synonym of timid, but the pair needs an antonym.", "2": "Fearful is close in meaning to timid.", "3": "Quiet is related to timid, not its opposite."}'),
  ('ENG-0503', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Word relationships (analogies)', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.3', 'Choose the pair that shows the same relationship as CHAPTER : BOOK.', '["Author : Novel", "Page : Ink", "Stanza : Poem", "Title : Cover"]', 2, 'A chapter is a section of a book; a stanza is a section of a poem (part : whole).', '{"0": "An author writes a novel (maker : product), not part : whole.", "1": "Ink is used on a page; it is not a whole made of pages.", "3": "A title appears on a cover; the relation is location, not part : whole."}'),
  ('ENG-0504', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Irony, parody and satire', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'In the sentence below, "sardonic" most nearly means:
"He gave a sardonic smile when his rival tripped on the stage."', '["mocking and scornful", "warm and kind", "nervous", "puzzled"]', 0, 'A sardonic smile is grimly mocking; smiling at a rival''s fall fits scorn, not kindness.', '{"1": "A kind smile would not fit pleasure at a rival''s embarrassment.", "2": "Nothing in the sentence suggests nervousness.", "3": "He clearly understood what happened."}'),
  ('ENG-0505', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Irony, parody and satire', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'A writer who uses humour and exaggeration to expose corruption among officials, hoping to bring about reform, is writing:', '["an elegy", "an ode", "satire", "a biography"]', 2, 'Satire uses humour, irony or exaggeration to criticise people''s vices or foolishness, usually to encourage change.', '{"0": "An elegy laments the dead.", "1": "An ode is a poem of praise.", "3": "A biography is a factual life story."}'),
  ('ENG-0506', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Irony, parody and satire', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'After his team lost 10-0, the coach said, "Well, that went brilliantly."
This remark is an example of:', '["verbal irony", "simile", "personification", "alliteration"]', 0, 'The coach says the opposite of what he means, which is verbal irony.', '{"1": "There is no comparison using ''like'' or ''as''.", "2": "Nothing non-human is given human qualities.", "3": "There is no repetition of first sounds."}'),
  ('ENG-0507', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Irony, parody and satire', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'A fire station burns down because of a faulty fire alarm.
This is an example of:', '["dramatic irony", "verbal irony", "hyperbole", "situational irony"]', 3, 'Situational irony is when the outcome is the opposite of what we would expect: a fire station is the last place expected to burn.', '{"0": "Dramatic irony needs an audience that knows something a character does not.", "1": "Verbal irony is something said; here it is an event.", "2": "Nothing is exaggerated."}'),
  ('ENG-0508', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Shades of meaning', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.4', 'Which word has the most negative connotation when describing a thin person?', '["slim", "scrawny", "slender", "lean"]', 1, '''Scrawny'' suggests unhealthily thin and bony; the others are neutral or flattering.', '{"0": "Slim is positive.", "2": "Slender suggests graceful thinness.", "3": "Lean suggests fit and healthy."}'),
  ('ENG-0509', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Irony, parody and satire', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'In the sentence below, "lampoon" most nearly means:
"Cartoonists in the weekly paper loved to lampoon the mayor''s endless broken promises."', '["to mock publicly through satire", "to praise warmly", "to interview formally", "to elect to office"]', 0, 'To lampoon is to ridicule someone, usually in satirical writing or drawings; broken promises are a typical target.', '{"1": "Cartoonists targeting broken promises are not praising.", "2": "Nothing suggests an interview.", "3": "Cartoonists do not elect anyone."}'),
  ('ENG-0510', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Shades of meaning', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.4', 'Which word describes a confident person in a NEGATIVE way?', '["self-assured", "assertive", "conceited", "poised"]', 2, '''Conceited'' means having too high an opinion of oneself; the others are neutral or positive.', '{"0": "Self-assured is positive.", "1": "Assertive is usually positive: confident in stating one''s views.", "3": "Poised suggests calm, graceful confidence."}'),
  ('ENG-0511', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Synonyms', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.4', 'Choose the word closest in meaning to "obdurate".', '["stubborn", "obedient", "obscure", "durable"]', 0, 'Obdurate means stubbornly refusing to change one''s opinion.', '{"1": "Obedient is close to the opposite.", "2": "Obscure means unclear; it only looks similar.", "3": "Durable means long-lasting; it only shares the letters ''dur''."}'),
  ('ENG-0512', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Antonyms', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.4', 'Choose the word most nearly OPPOSITE in meaning to "ephemeral".', '["brief", "ethereal", "permanent", "fragile"]', 2, 'Ephemeral means lasting a very short time; permanent is the opposite.', '{"0": "Brief is a synonym.", "1": "Ethereal means delicate and light; it only sounds similar.", "3": "Fragile is related in feeling but not an opposite."}'),
