  ('ENG-0531', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Imagery and the senses', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.3', 'A writer uses sensory imagery mainly to:', '["state facts as briefly as possible", "prove an argument with evidence", "make the lines rhyme", "help the reader see, hear or feel the scene"]', 3, 'Imagery appeals to the senses so that the reader experiences the scene, not just reads about it.', '{"0": "Imagery adds detail; it is not mainly about brevity.", "1": "Evidence and argument are not the purpose of imagery.", "2": "Rhyme is a sound pattern, unrelated to imagery."}'),
  ('ENG-0532', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Scanning a passage', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 6.2', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
Antibiotics kill bacteria or stop them from multiplying, but they have no effect on viruses, which is why they do not cure the common cold. When antibiotics are taken too often or stopped too early, the bacteria that survive are usually the hardiest ones. These survivors multiply and pass on their resistance, so a drug that once worked may slowly become useless. Doctors therefore advise patients to finish the full course prescribed, and never to share or save leftover tablets. Some countries now limit the use of antibiotics in farm animals as well, since resistant bacteria can spread from animals to people.
