The northern areas of Pakistan hold more glacial ice than almost anywhere outside the polar regions. In summer, meltwater from these glaciers feeds the Indus and its tributaries, supplying water to farms hundreds of kilometres downstream. As temperatures rise, many glaciers are shrinking, and lakes form behind loose walls of rock and ice. If such a wall gives way, the lake can empty in minutes, sending a flood through the valleys below. This event, called a glacial lake outburst flood, gives villagers little warning, which is why early-warning systems have been installed in several valleys.
