  ('ENG-0540', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Tone and purpose', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 6.1', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
For decades, the placebo was treated as a nuisance, a sugar pill whose only job was to show how much better a real drug performed. Yet the ''nuisance'' has proved stubborn. In some trials of pain relief, patients given dummy tablets report improvements nearly as large as those given the active drug, and brain scans show that their bodies release natural painkillers. None of this means that belief can shrink a tumour or mend a broken bone; the effect is strongest for symptoms that the brain itself helps to produce, such as pain, nausea and fatigue. What the findings do suggest is that the ritual of care, the confident doctor and the careful explanation, may be part of the treatment rather than mere decoration around it.
