What is the writer''s main purpose?', '["To argue that the placebo effect is real but limited, and that the manner of care matters", "To show that placebos can cure any disease", "To criticise doctors for using sugar pills", "To explain step by step how drug trials are run"]', 0, 'The passage presents evidence for real effects, sets limits (tumours, bones) and concludes that the ritual of care is part of treatment.', '{"1": "The writer clearly limits the effect.", "2": "No criticism of doctors is made.", "3": "Trials are mentioned only as evidence."}'),
  ('ENG-0545', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Scanning a passage', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 6.2', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
Ibn al-Haytham, who worked in Cairo in the eleventh century, is often called a pioneer of the experimental method. Earlier thinkers had argued about vision mainly through reasoning, and some held that the eye sends out rays that touch the objects it sees. Ibn al-Haytham insisted that such claims be tested. Using a darkened room with a small hole in one wall, he showed that light from outside travels in straight lines and forms an inverted image on the opposite wall. From this and other experiments he concluded that we see because light enters the eye, not because the eye sends light out. His insistence that a theory must answer to observation, however elegant the argument behind it, is the habit of mind on which modern science rests.
According to the passage, what did some earlier thinkers believe about vision?', '["Light enters the eye from objects.", "Light travels in curved paths.", "Images form upside down in the eye.", "The eye sends out rays that touch objects."]', 3, 'The passage says "some held that the eye sends out rays that touch the objects it sees".', '{"0": "This was Ibn al-Haytham''s conclusion, not the earlier view.", "1": "This is not mentioned.", "2": "This is not given as an earlier belief."}'),
  ('ENG-0546', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Scanning a passage', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 6.2', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
Ibn al-Haytham, who worked in Cairo in the eleventh century, is often called a pioneer of the experimental method. Earlier thinkers had argued about vision mainly through reasoning, and some held that the eye sends out rays that touch the objects it sees. Ibn al-Haytham insisted that such claims be tested. Using a darkened room with a small hole in one wall, he showed that light from outside travels in straight lines and forms an inverted image on the opposite wall. From this and other experiments he concluded that we see because light enters the eye, not because the eye sends light out. His insistence that a theory must answer to observation, however elegant the argument behind it, is the habit of mind on which modern science rests.
What did the darkened-room experiment show?', '["The eye sends out rays of light.", "Light travels in straight lines and forms an inverted image.", "Light bends around corners.", "Images always appear the right way up."]', 1, 'The passage says the light "travels in straight lines and forms an inverted image on the opposite wall".', '{"0": "The experiment led him to reject this.", "2": "This contradicts the straight-line result.", "3": "The image was inverted."}'),
  ('ENG-0547', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.2; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 1.1', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
Ibn al-Haytham, who worked in Cairo in the eleventh century, is often called a pioneer of the experimental method. Earlier thinkers had argued about vision mainly through reasoning, and some held that the eye sends out rays that touch the objects it sees. Ibn al-Haytham insisted that such claims be tested. Using a darkened room with a small hole in one wall, he showed that light from outside travels in straight lines and forms an inverted image on the opposite wall. From this and other experiments he concluded that we see because light enters the eye, not because the eye sends light out. His insistence that a theory must answer to observation, however elegant the argument behind it, is the habit of mind on which modern science rests.
As used in the last sentence, "elegant" most nearly means:', '["fashionably dressed", "neat and pleasing in its reasoning", "very long", "foreign"]', 1, 'It describes ''the argument'', so it means neat and attractive in reasoning, not stylish in appearance.', '{"0": "This sense applies to people and clothes, not arguments.", "2": "Length is not the meaning.", "3": "Unrelated to the word."}'),
  ('ENG-0548', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Tone and purpose', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 6.1', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
Ibn al-Haytham, who worked in Cairo in the eleventh century, is often called a pioneer of the experimental method. Earlier thinkers had argued about vision mainly through reasoning, and some held that the eye sends out rays that touch the objects it sees. Ibn al-Haytham insisted that such claims be tested. Using a darkened room with a small hole in one wall, he showed that light from outside travels in straight lines and forms an inverted image on the opposite wall. From this and other experiments he concluded that we see because light enters the eye, not because the eye sends light out. His insistence that a theory must answer to observation, however elegant the argument behind it, is the habit of mind on which modern science rests.
The writer''s attitude towards Ibn al-Haytham is best described as:', '["sceptical", "mocking", "indifferent", "admiring"]', 3, 'He is called a pioneer, and his habit of mind is said to be the basis of modern science.', '{"0": "The writer does not doubt his achievements.", "1": "There is no ridicule.", "2": "The final sentence shows strong approval."}'),
  ('ENG-0549', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Inference from a passage', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.1; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 6.1', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
Ibn al-Haytham, who worked in Cairo in the eleventh century, is often called a pioneer of the experimental method. Earlier thinkers had argued about vision mainly through reasoning, and some held that the eye sends out rays that touch the objects it sees. Ibn al-Haytham insisted that such claims be tested. Using a darkened room with a small hole in one wall, he showed that light from outside travels in straight lines and forms an inverted image on the opposite wall. From this and other experiments he concluded that we see because light enters the eye, not because the eye sends light out. His insistence that a theory must answer to observation, however elegant the argument behind it, is the habit of mind on which modern science rests.
Which idea is assumed in the final sentence?', '["Modern science values testing ideas against observation.", "All earlier theories about vision were foolish.", "Reasoning has no place in science.", "Cairo is still the centre of world science."]', 0, 'The sentence says that making theory ''answer to observation'' is the habit on which modern science rests.', '{"1": "The writer does not call them foolish, only untested.", "2": "The writer values testing, not the end of reasoning.", "3": "Nothing suggests this."}'),
  ('ENG-0550', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Figures of speech', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.3', '"The White House announced new sanctions today."
The phrase "The White House" is an example of:', '["personification", "hyperbole", "metonymy", "simile"]', 2, 'Metonymy names something by a thing closely linked to it: the building stands for the US government.', '{"0": "The point is substitution of a related name, not giving a building human qualities.", "1": "Nothing is exaggerated.", "3": "There is no ''like'' or ''as'' comparison."}'),
  ('ENG-0551', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Figures of speech', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.3', 'In "The captain called for all hands on deck," the word "hands" (meaning sailors) is an example of:', '["simile", "alliteration", "synecdoche", "irony"]', 2, 'Synecdoche uses a part (hands) to stand for the whole (the sailors).', '{"0": "No comparison with ''like'' or ''as'' is made.", "1": "There is no repetition of first sounds.", "3": "Nothing is meant to be the opposite."}'),
  ('ENG-0552', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Figures of speech', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.3', 'Which phrase is an oxymoron?', '["deafening silence", "silent night", "loud noise", "cold ice"]', 0, 'An oxymoron puts two contradictory words side by side: silence cannot literally deafen.', '{"1": "The words do not contradict each other.", "2": "The words agree.", "3": "The words agree."}'),
  ('ENG-0553', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Figures of speech', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.3', '"The more you learn, the more you realise how little you know."
This statement is best described as:', '["an oxymoron", "a simile", "onomatopoeia", "a paradox"]', 3, 'A paradox is a statement that seems self-contradictory but contains a truth.', '{"0": "An oxymoron is two contradictory words placed together, not a whole contradictory-seeming statement.", "1": "There is no comparison.", "2": "No word imitates a sound."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0554', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Figures of speech', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.3', 'After running a marathon in 40-degree heat, she said, "I''m a little tired."
This is an example of:', '["hyperbole", "understatement", "simile", "personification"]', 1, 'She deliberately makes her exhaustion sound much smaller than it is.', '{"0": "Hyperbole exaggerates; this does the reverse.", "2": "There is no comparison.", "3": "Nothing non-human is given human qualities."}'),
  ('ENG-0555', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Irony, parody and satire', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.3', 'In a play, the audience knows that the cup contains poison, but the king drinks from it happily.
This is an example of:', '["situational irony", "dramatic irony", "verbal irony", "satire"]', 1, 'Dramatic irony is when the audience knows something important that a character does not.', '{"0": "Situational irony is an unexpected outcome; here the point is the gap in knowledge.", "2": "No one says the opposite of what they mean.", "3": "Satire mocks vices in society."}'),
  ('ENG-0556', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Neither...nor and either...or', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct pronoun:
Neither the head nurse nor the junior doctors had brought ______ notes.', '["her", "his", "its", "their"]', 3, 'With neither...nor, the pronoun agrees with the nearer antecedent, "the junior doctors", which is plural.', '{"0": "\"Her\" would agree with \"head nurse\", which is the farther antecedent.", "1": "The nearer antecedent is plural.", "2": "\"Its\" is for things."}'),
  ('ENG-0557', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Pronoun case', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Which sentence means "She likes you more than I like you"?', '["She likes you more than I do.", "She likes you more than me.", "She likes you more than she likes me.", "She likes you more than me do."]', 0, '"I do" makes it clear that "I" is the subject of a second "like".', '{"1": "This more naturally means \"more than she likes me\".", "2": "This compares her liking for you and for me.", "3": "\"Me\" cannot be the subject of \"do\"."}'),
  ('ENG-0558', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Pronoun case', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct pronoun:
Between you and ______, the results were disappointing.', '["I", "myself", "me", "mine"]', 2, '"Between" is a preposition, so both pronouns after it must be in the object case.', '{"0": "\"I\" is a subject pronoun and cannot follow a preposition.", "1": "A reflexive pronoun needs an antecedent \"I\" in the same clause.", "3": "\"Mine\" is a possessive pronoun."}'),
  ('ENG-0559', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Relative pronouns', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct pronoun:
The doctor ______ I thought would operate was on leave.', '["who", "whom", "whose", "which"]', 0, 'Remove "I thought": "the doctor who would operate". The pronoun is the subject of "would operate", so "who".', '{"1": "\"Whom\" is an object form, but the pronoun is the subject of \"would operate\".", "2": "\"Whose\" shows possession.", "3": "\"Which\" is not used for people."}'),
  ('ENG-0560', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Relative pronouns', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct pronoun:
Give the prize to ______ solves the puzzle first.', '["whomever", "whom", "who ever", "whoever"]', 3, 'The whole clause "whoever solves the puzzle first" is the object of "to"; inside it, the pronoun is the subject of "solves".', '{"0": "The pronoun is the subject of \"solves\", so the subject form is needed.", "1": "\"Whom\" cannot be the subject of \"solves\".", "2": "It is written as one word."}'),
  ('ENG-0561', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Clear pronoun reference', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Which sentence has an unclear pronoun reference?', '["When Ali met Bilal, Ali was nervous.", "When Ali met Bilal, he was nervous.", "Ali was nervous when he met Bilal.", "Bilal was calm, but Ali was nervous when they met."]', 1, '"He" could mean Ali or Bilal.', '{"0": "The name is repeated, so there is no doubt.", "2": "\"He\" clearly refers back to the subject, Ali.", "3": "Both people are named."}'),
  ('ENG-0562', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Reflexive pronouns', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Which sentence uses a reflexive pronoun correctly?', '["Please send the report to Sara or myself.", "The surgeon reminded herself to check the instruments.", "My brother and myself attended the seminar.", "The team and themselves celebrated."]', 1, 'A reflexive pronoun is correct when it refers back to the subject of the same clause: the surgeon reminded herself.', '{"0": "\"Myself\" has no \"I\" in the clause to refer to; use \"me\".", "2": "A subject needs \"I\", not \"myself\".", "3": "\"Themselves\" cannot be part of a subject."}'),
  ('ENG-0563', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Relative pronouns', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct word:
The textbook, ______ I borrowed from the library, is overdue.', '["that", "who", "which", "what"]', 2, 'A non-defining clause (set off by commas) uses "which" for things, never "that".', '{"0": "\"That\" is not used in non-defining relative clauses.", "1": "\"Who\" is for people.", "3": "\"What\" cannot introduce a relative clause after a noun."}'),
  ('ENG-0564', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Relative pronouns', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct word:
The patient ______ X-ray was missing had to wait for another scan.', '["who''s", "whom", "whose", "which"]', 2, '"Whose" is the possessive relative pronoun: the X-ray belonged to the patient.', '{"0": "\"Who''s\" means \"who is\" or \"who has\".", "1": "\"Whom\" is an object pronoun and cannot show possession.", "3": "\"Which\" is not used for people."}'),
  ('ENG-0565', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Relative pronouns', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct word for formal written English:
The candidate ______ the panel selected has ten years'' experience.', '["who", "whose", "whom", "which"]', 2, 'The pronoun is the object of "selected" (the panel selected him or her), so the object form "whom" is used.', '{"0": "In formal English the object form is needed; \"who\" is the subject form.", "1": "\"Whose\" shows possession.", "3": "\"Which\" is not used for people."}'),
  ('ENG-0566', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Pronoun case', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct pronoun:
Let you and ______ settle this matter ourselves.', '["I", "myself", "me", "mine"]', 2, '"Let" takes an object, so both pronouns after it are in the object case: you and me.', '{"0": "\"I\" is a subject pronoun and cannot be the object of \"let\".", "1": "A reflexive needs \"I\" as the subject of the same clause.", "3": "\"Mine\" is possessive."}'),
  ('ENG-0567', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Pronoun case', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct pronoun:
Everyone except ______ passed the test.', '["him", "he", "his", "himself"]', 0, '"Except" is a preposition here, so the object pronoun follows.', '{"1": "A subject pronoun cannot follow a preposition.", "2": "\"His\" is possessive.", "3": "A reflexive needs an antecedent in the clause."}'),
  ('ENG-0568', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Reflexive pronouns', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct word:
The principal ______ handed out the prizes, which delighted the students.', '["her", "hers", "she", "herself"]', 3, 'An emphatic (intensive) pronoun placed after the subject stresses that she did it in person.', '{"0": "\"Her\" is an object or possessive form and cannot follow the subject like this.", "1": "\"Hers\" is a possessive pronoun.", "2": "Repeating \"she\" after the subject is not correct."}'),
  ('ENG-0569', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Wishes and unreal past', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
I wish I ______ the answer now.', '["know", "knew", "will know", "have known"]', 1, 'A wish about the present uses the past simple: ''I wish I knew''.', '{"0": "The present simple is not used after ''wish''.", "2": "''Will'' is not used after ''wish'' for this meaning.", "3": "The present perfect is not used after ''wish''."}'),
  ('ENG-0570', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Wishes and unreal past', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
She wishes she ______ harder last year.', '["studied", "has studied", "would study", "had studied"]', 3, 'A wish about the past uses the past perfect.', '{"0": "The past simple after wish refers to the present.", "1": "The present perfect is not used after wish.", "2": "''Would'' after wish refers to the future or to someone else''s habit."}'),
  ('ENG-0571', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Conditionals', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
If I ______ you, I would see a doctor.', '["were", "am", "was being", "will be"]', 0, 'In unreal present conditions, formal English uses ''were'' for all persons.', '{"1": "The present tense is used for real conditions.", "2": "The continuous form is wrong here.", "3": "''Will'' is not used in the if-clause."}'),
  ('ENG-0572', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Conditionals', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
If she had taken the medicine yesterday, she ______ better now.', '["would have felt", "will feel", "would feel", "felt"]', 2, 'This is a mixed conditional: a past condition (had taken) with a present result (now), so ''would + base verb''.', '{"0": "This refers to a past result, but ''now'' shows the result is in the present.", "1": "This is a first conditional result and does not match an unreal past condition.", "3": "The result clause needs ''would''."}'),
  ('ENG-0573', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Wishes and unreal past', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
It''s high time we ______ for the airport.', '["left", "leave", "will leave", "are leaving"]', 0, '''It''s (high) time'' + subject is followed by the past simple, which here refers to the present.', '{"1": "The base form is not used after ''it''s high time we''.", "2": "Future forms are not used here.", "3": "The present continuous is not used here."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0574', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Subjunctive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
The doctor insisted that the patient ______ in bed for two days.', '["stays", "stayed", "will stay", "stay"]', 3, 'After verbs of insisting, demanding or recommending, the subjunctive uses the base form for all persons (''that the patient stay''). British English may also use ''should stay''.', '{"0": "The subjunctive does not add -s.", "1": "The past form is not the subjunctive here.", "2": "''Will'' is not used after ''insisted that''."}'),
  ('ENG-0575', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Used to and be used to', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
When I was a child, I ______ in Quetta, but now I live in Karachi.', '["used to live", "am used to living", "was used to live", "use to live"]', 0, '''Used to + base verb'' describes a past situation that is no longer true.', '{"1": "This means being accustomed to living somewhere now.", "2": "After \"was used to\", a gerund is needed, and the meaning would be different.", "3": "In a positive past statement the form is \"used to\"."}'),
  ('ENG-0576', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Past perfect continuous', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
By the time the ambulance arrived, the patient ______ for an hour.', '["has been waiting", "was waiting", "waited", "had been waiting"]', 3, 'An action that continued up to a point in the past uses the past perfect continuous.', '{"0": "The present perfect continuous connects to now, not to a past moment.", "1": "The past continuous does not show duration up to that point with \"for an hour\".", "2": "The simple past does not show the waiting continuing up to the arrival."}'),
  ('ENG-0577', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Present perfect', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
This is the first time I ______ a live operation.', '["watch", "have watched", "watched", "am watching"]', 1, 'After "This is the first time", the present perfect is used.', '{"0": "The present simple is not used after this structure.", "2": "The simple past is used after \"It was the first time\", not \"This is\".", "3": "The pattern requires the present perfect."}'),
  ('ENG-0578', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Future perfect continuous', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
By next June, she ______ at this hospital for ten years.', '["will work", "will have been working", "will be working", "has been working"]', 1, 'An action continuing up to a future point, with a stated duration, uses the future perfect continuous.', '{"0": "The simple future does not show duration up to a point.", "2": "The future continuous does not show the length of time up to that point.", "3": "This connects to now, not to next June."}'),
  ('ENG-0579', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Conditionals', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
If he ______ the warning, the accident would not have happened.', '["had heeded", "heeded", "would have heeded", "has heeded"]', 0, 'A third conditional (unreal past) uses the past perfect in the if-clause.', '{"1": "The simple past is for the second conditional.", "2": "\"Would\" does not go in the if-clause.", "3": "The present perfect is not used here."}'),
  ('ENG-0580', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Conditionals', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
Unless you ______ the dose, the fever will not come down.', '["will increase", "increased", "increase", "would increase"]', 2, '"Unless" (= if not) is followed by the present simple when the result is in the future.', '{"0": "\"Will\" is not used after \"unless\" in a first conditional.", "1": "The past form does not match \"will\" in the main clause.", "3": "\"Would\" does not match the first conditional."}'),
  ('ENG-0581', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Stative verbs', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
I ______ him since we were at school together.', '["have known", "know", "am knowing", "knew"]', 0, '"Since" plus a stative verb ("know") takes the present perfect simple.', '{"1": "The present simple cannot be used with \"since\".", "2": "\"Know\" is stative and not used in the continuous.", "3": "The simple past does not link to the present."}'),
  ('ENG-0582', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Past continuous', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
When I entered the ward, the nurse ______ the patient''s temperature, so I waited.', '["took", "has taken", "had been took", "was taking"]', 3, 'The past continuous shows an action already in progress when another happened; that is why I waited.', '{"0": "\"Took\" would mean she started after I entered, so there would be nothing to wait for.", "1": "The present perfect does not fit a past story.", "2": "This is not a correct verb form."}'),
  ('ENG-0583', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Future time clauses', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
I will call you as soon as I ______ the report.', '["will have read", "have read", "had read", "would read"]', 1, 'In time clauses about the future, the present perfect stresses that the action will be complete first; "will" is not used.', '{"0": "\"Will\" is not used in a time clause after \"as soon as\".", "2": "The past perfect does not fit a future meaning.", "3": "\"Would\" is not used here."}'),
  ('ENG-0584', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Conditionals', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
Were I ______ the post, I would refuse it.', '["offer", "to be offered", "offered", "to offer"]', 1, '"Were I to be offered" is the inverted form of "If I were to be offered", a formal hypothetical.', '{"0": "I am not the one offering.", "2": "After \"Were I\", a to-infinitive is needed.", "3": "This would mean I am offering the post."}'),
  ('ENG-0585', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Wishes and unreal past', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
I would rather you ______ the details tonight, not tomorrow.', '["finish", "will finish", "have finished", "finished"]', 3, '"Would rather" + another subject is followed by the past simple to talk about the present or future.', '{"0": "With a different subject, the past form is normally used.", "1": "\"Will\" is not used here.", "2": "The present perfect is not used here."}'),
  ('ENG-0586', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Conditionals', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
She ______ to the lecture if she had not been ill.', '["would go", "will go", "had gone", "would have gone"]', 3, 'The if-clause is an unreal past (had not been), so the result is "would have + past participle".', '{"0": "This is a second-conditional result.", "1": "This is a first-conditional result.", "2": "The result clause needs \"would\"."}'),
  ('ENG-0587', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Stative verbs', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
This parcel ______ two kilograms.', '["is weighing", "weighs", "has been weighing", "weighing"]', 1, 'When "weigh" describes how heavy something is, it is stative and is not used in the continuous.', '{"0": "The continuous is used only when someone is actively weighing something.", "2": "This also uses the active sense.", "3": "The sentence needs a finite verb."}'),
  ('ENG-0588', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Future continuous', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
Please don''t call me at nine; I ______ my exam then.', '["will take", "will be taking", "take", "will have taken"]', 1, 'The future continuous shows an action in progress at a particular future time.', '{"0": "The simple future does not show the exam being in progress at nine.", "2": "The present simple does not fit a specific future moment.", "3": "This means the exam will be over by nine."}'),
  ('ENG-0589', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Past perfect', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
The patient told us that he ______ any food since the previous night.', '["has not eaten", "did not eat", "was not eating", "had not eaten"]', 3, 'An action continuing up to a past moment (when he told us), with "since", takes the past perfect.', '{"0": "The present perfect connects to now, not to a past moment.", "1": "The simple past does not work with \"since\".", "2": "The past continuous does not work with \"since\"."}'),
  ('ENG-0590', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Perfect and passive infinitives', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
She seems ______ the answer already; look how she is smiling.', '["to find", "to have found", "finding", "to be found"]', 1, 'The perfect infinitive (to have + past participle) shows an action completed before the time of ''seems''; ''already'' signals this.', '{"0": "The simple infinitive does not show a completed earlier action.", "2": "''Seem'' is followed by an infinitive, not a gerund.", "3": "This is passive: she would be the thing found."}'),
  ('ENG-0591', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Perfect and passive infinitives', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
The forms have ______ by Friday.', '["to submit", "to be submitted", "submitting", "be submitted"]', 1, '''Have to'' + passive infinitive: the forms are not doing the submitting; someone must submit them.', '{"0": "The forms cannot submit anything; the passive is needed.", "2": "''Have to'' needs an infinitive, and ''have submitting'' is not a form.", "3": "After ''have'' meaning obligation, ''to'' is needed."}'),
  ('ENG-0592', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Functions of the gerund', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'In "Swimming strengthens the heart," the gerund "Swimming" functions as the:', '["object", "subject complement", "object of a preposition", "subject"]', 3, '''Swimming'' is what strengthens the heart, so it is the subject of the verb.', '{"0": "The object is \"the heart\".", "1": "There is no linking verb.", "2": "There is no preposition before it."}'),
  ('ENG-0593', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Gerund with a possessive', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct word:
The doctor objected to ______ leaving the hospital so early.', '["he", "himself", "his", "he''s"]', 2, 'A possessive pronoun normally comes before a gerund in formal English: his leaving.', '{"0": "A subject pronoun cannot come after the preposition \"to\".", "1": "The reflexive pronoun would refer back to the doctor and is wrong here.", "3": "''He''s'' means ''he is'' or ''he has''."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0594', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Functions of the infinitive', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
She went to the library ______ for the exam.', '["for preparing", "for prepare", "to prepare", "preparing"]', 2, 'Purpose is shown by the infinitive: she went there in order to prepare.', '{"0": "''For + -ing'' describes the use of a thing, not a person''s purpose.", "1": "''For'' cannot be followed by the base verb.", "3": "This does not express purpose."}'),
  ('ENG-0595', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Functions of the infinitive', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'In "To become a surgeon requires years of training," the infinitive phrase is the:', '["subject", "object", "adverb", "adjective"]', 0, '''To become a surgeon'' is what requires years of training.', '{"1": "The object is \"years of training\".", "2": "It does not modify the verb.", "3": "It does not describe a noun."}'),
  ('ENG-0596', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Functions of the infinitive', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
The box is ______ for me to lift.', '["so heavy", "too heavy", "very heavy", "much heavy"]', 1, '''Too + adjective + for someone + to-infinitive'' means it is beyond what is possible.', '{"0": "''So'' is followed by ''that'', not ''for me to''.", "2": "''Very'' does not link to an infinitive of result.", "3": "''Much'' is not used before a plain adjective."}'),
  ('ENG-0597', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Functions of the gerund', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'In "The doctor recommended walking every morning," the gerund phrase "walking every morning" is used as:', '["the subject", "the direct object", "a subject complement", "the object of a preposition"]', 1, 'It answers ''recommended what?'', so it is the object of the verb.', '{"0": "The subject is \"The doctor\".", "2": "There is no linking verb.", "3": "No preposition comes before it."}'),
  ('ENG-0598', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Functions of the infinitive', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'In "She was happy to help," the infinitive "to help" modifies:', '["the pronoun \"She\"", "nothing; it is the subject", "nothing; it is the object of \"was\"", "the adjective \"happy\""]', 3, 'It explains in what way she was happy, so it works adverbially on the adjective.', '{"0": "It does not describe her directly.", "1": "The subject is \"She\".", "2": "\"Was\" is a linking verb and takes no object."}'),
  ('ENG-0599', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Change of meaning: gerund or infinitive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
I clearly remember ______ the door, but now it is open!', '["to lock", "lock", "locking", "to have locking"]', 2, '"Remember + -ing" refers to a memory of something done earlier.', '{"0": "\"Remember to\" means not forgetting a future task.", "1": "\"Remember\" is not followed by a bare infinitive.", "3": "This is not a correct form."}'),
  ('ENG-0600', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Change of meaning: gerund or infinitive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
She tried ______ the window, but it was stuck.', '["opening", "open", "to have opened", "to open"]', 3, '"Try + to-infinitive" means to make an attempt; "but it was stuck" shows the attempt failed.', '{"0": "\"Try + -ing\" means doing something as an experiment, which assumes you can do it.", "1": "\"Try\" is not followed by a bare infinitive.", "2": "The perfect infinitive does not fit an attempt."}'),
  ('ENG-0601', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Change of meaning: gerund or infinitive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
We regret ______ you that your application has been unsuccessful.', '["informing", "to inform", "inform", "to informing"]', 1, '"Regret + to-infinitive" is used when giving bad news now.', '{"0": "\"Regret + -ing\" means being sorry about something already done.", "2": "\"Regret\" is not followed by a bare infinitive.", "3": "This is not a correct form."}'),
  ('ENG-0602', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Bare infinitive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
He was made ______ the medicine.', '["take", "taking", "taken", "to take"]', 3, 'In the passive, "make" is followed by a to-infinitive.', '{"0": "The bare infinitive is used only in the active voice.", "1": "This is not used after the passive of \"make\".", "2": "This is not a correct form here."}'),
  ('ENG-0603', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Bare infinitive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
I''d rather ______ at home tonight.', '["to stay", "staying", "stay", "stayed"]', 2, '"Would rather" is followed by the bare infinitive.', '{"0": "\"To\" is not used after \"would rather\".", "1": "\"Would rather\" is not followed by -ing.", "3": "The past form is used only with a different subject."}'),
  ('ENG-0604', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Verbs followed by a gerund', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
She denied ______ the files.', '["having deleted", "to delete", "to have deleted", "delete"]', 0, '"Deny" is followed by a gerund; the perfect gerund stresses the action was in the past.', '{"1": "\"Deny\" is not followed by an infinitive.", "2": "\"Deny\" is not followed by an infinitive.", "3": "A bare verb does not fit."}'),
  ('ENG-0605', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Functions of the infinitive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the best completion:
To pass the exam, ______.', '["every chapter must be revised", "hard work is essential", "the syllabus must be covered", "students must revise every chapter"]', 3, 'An introductory infinitive phrase must refer to the subject of the main clause; students are the ones passing the exam.', '{"0": "This makes \"every chapter\" the one passing the exam.", "1": "\"Hard work\" cannot pass the exam.", "2": "The syllabus does not pass the exam."}'),
  ('ENG-0606', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Perfect and passive infinitives', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
He is said ______ the company in 2010.', '["to found", "to have founded", "founding", "to be founding"]', 1, 'The founding happened before the time of saying, so the perfect infinitive is used.', '{"0": "The simple infinitive does not fit a past date.", "2": "\"Is said\" is followed by a to-infinitive.", "3": "This is not a correct form for a past action."}'),
  ('ENG-0607', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Verbs followed by an infinitive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct sentence.', '["She can''t afford buying a new laptop.", "She can''t afford to buy a new laptop.", "She can''t afford buy a new laptop.", "She can''t afford for buy a new laptop."]', 1, '"Afford" is followed by a to-infinitive.', '{"0": "\"Afford\" is not followed by a gerund.", "2": "\"Afford\" needs \"to\".", "3": "\"For\" is not used here."}'),
  ('ENG-0608', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Change of meaning: gerund or infinitive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
I meant ______ you yesterday, but I forgot.', '["to call", "calling", "call", "to calling"]', 0, '"Mean + to-infinitive" means to intend.', '{"1": "\"Mean + -ing\" means to involve, as in \"Missing the bus means waiting\".", "2": "\"Mean\" is not followed by a bare infinitive.", "3": "This is not a correct form."}'),
  ('ENG-0609', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Change of meaning: gerund or infinitive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
Missing the last bus means ______ for an hour.', '["to wait", "wait", "waiting", "waited"]', 2, '"Mean + -ing" means to involve or result in.', '{"0": "\"Mean + to\" means to intend, which does not fit.", "1": "A bare verb does not follow \"means\".", "3": "A past form does not fit."}'),
  ('ENG-0610', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Gerund after a preposition', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
I object to ______ treated like a child.', '["be", "been", "being", "have been"]', 2, 'In "object to", "to" is a preposition, so a gerund follows; the passive gerund is "being + past participle".', '{"0": "A base verb cannot follow a preposition.", "1": "A past participle cannot follow \"to\" here.", "3": "A perfect infinitive does not follow \"object to\"."}'),
  ('ENG-0611', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Change of meaning: gerund or infinitive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5, 2.6; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct form:
Even after we asked her to stop, she went on ______ about her new job.', '["talking", "to talk", "talk", "talked"]', 0, '"Go on + -ing" means to continue doing the same thing.', '{"1": "\"Go on + to\" means to move on to a new activity.", "2": "A bare verb does not fit.", "3": "A past form does not fit."}'),
  ('ENG-0612', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Adjective or adverb', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Choose the correct word:
The patient arrived ______ for his appointment.', '["lately", "latter", "late", "latest"]', 2, '"Late" is the adverb meaning after the expected time.', '{"0": "\"Lately\" means recently.", "1": "\"Latter\" means the second of two.", "3": "\"Latest\" means most recent or last of all."}'),
  ('ENG-0613', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Adjective or adverb', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Choose the correct word:
I feel ______ about missing your wedding.', '["bad", "badly", "worst", "badness"]', 0, '"Feel" is a linking verb here, so it takes an adjective describing the subject.', '{"1": "\"Badly\" would describe how well I perform the act of feeling.", "2": "Nothing is being compared.", "3": "A noun does not fit."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0614', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Kinds of adverbs', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'In "Where did you put the slides?", the word "where" is:', '["a relative adverb", "a pronoun", "a preposition", "an interrogative adverb"]', 3, 'It asks a question about place, so it is an interrogative adverb.', '{"0": "A relative adverb joins a clause to a noun, as in \"the place where\".", "1": "It asks about place; it does not stand for a noun.", "2": "It has no object."}'),
  ('ENG-0615', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Kinds of adverbs', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'In "This is the lab where we tested the samples," the word "where" is:', '["an interrogative adverb", "a relative adverb", "a preposition", "a demonstrative"]', 1, 'It introduces a relative clause describing the noun "lab".', '{"0": "The sentence does not ask a question.", "2": "It has no object.", "3": "It does not point out a noun."}'),
  ('ENG-0616', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Kinds of adverbs', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'In which sentence is "hopefully" used as a sentence adverb that comments on the whole statement?', '["She waited hopefully by the door.", "Hopefully, the results will be ready by Monday.", "He looked hopefully at the screen.", "They watched hopefully as the scores came in."]', 1, 'Here "hopefully" means "it is hoped that" and modifies the whole sentence.', '{"0": "Here it describes how she waited (manner).", "2": "Here it describes how he looked (manner).", "3": "Here it describes how they watched (manner)."}'),
  ('ENG-0617', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Position of adverbs', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Choose the correct sentence.', '["Is the water enough warm to bathe the baby?", "Is enough the water warm to bathe the baby?", "Is the water warm to bathe enough the baby?", "Is the water warm enough to bathe the baby?"]', 3, '"Enough" as an adverb comes after the adjective it modifies.', '{"0": "\"Enough\" follows the adjective.", "1": "\"Enough\" cannot come before the subject here.", "2": "\"Enough\" is misplaced."}'),
  ('ENG-0618', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Kinds of adverbs', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Choose the correct word:
She ______ ever misses a lecture.', '["hard", "seldomly", "hardly", "scarce"]', 2, '"Hardly ever" means almost never.', '{"0": "\"Hard ever\" is not a phrase.", "1": "\"Seldom\" has no -ly form in standard English.", "3": "\"Scarce\" is an adjective; the adverb is \"scarcely\"."}'),
  ('ENG-0619', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Position of adverbs', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Choose the correct sentence.', '["She always has been interested in medicine always.", "Always she has been interested in medicine.", "She has always been interested in medicine.", "She has been always interested in medicine."]', 2, 'Adverbs of frequency usually go after the first auxiliary verb.', '{"0": "\"Always\" is repeated.", "1": "Fronting \"always\" like this is not normal word order.", "3": "\"Always\" goes after the first auxiliary (\"has\")."}'),
  ('ENG-0620', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Position changes meaning', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Which sentence means that the professor, of all people, could not solve the problem?', '["The professor could not even solve the problem.", "The professor could not solve even the problem.", "The professor even could not solve the problem.", "Even the professor could not solve the problem."]', 3, '"Even" placed before "the professor" stresses that this is surprising for someone like him.', '{"0": "This stresses the problem: he could not manage even this.", "1": "\"Even\" here points to \"the problem\", not the professor.", "2": "This is not natural word order."}'),
  ('ENG-0621', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Kinds of adverbs', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Choose the correct word:
I haven''t finished my report ______.', '["already", "yet", "still", "ever"]', 1, '"Yet" is used at the end of negative sentences and questions for something expected to happen.', '{"0": "\"Already\" is used in positive sentences.", "2": "\"Still\" goes before the negative: \"I still haven''t finished\".", "3": "\"Ever\" means at any time and does not fit here."}'),
  ('ENG-0622', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Kinds of adverbs', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Choose the correct word:
He is ______ waiting for his results; they have not come.', '["yet", "still", "already", "ever"]', 1, '"Still" shows that an action is continuing.', '{"0": "\"Yet\" is used in negatives and questions, usually at the end.", "2": "\"Already\" means sooner than expected.", "3": "\"Ever\" means at any time."}'),
  ('ENG-0623', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Adjective or adverb', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'In which sentence is "fast" used as an adverb?', '["She is a fast runner.", "These colours are fast.", "They broke their fast at sunset.", "She runs fast."]', 3, 'Here "fast" tells how she runs, so it modifies the verb.', '{"0": "Here \"fast\" describes the noun \"runner\" (adjective).", "1": "Here \"fast\" means not fading, describing \"colours\" (adjective).", "2": "Here \"fast\" is a noun."}'),
  ('ENG-0624', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Fixed prepositions', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.3', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
All forms must be submitted prior ______ the deadline.', '["than", "before", "from", "to"]', 3, '"Prior to" is the fixed form; like "senior to" and "superior to", it never takes "than".', '{"0": "\"Prior than\" is a common error.", "1": "\"Prior before\" repeats the same idea twice.", "2": "\"Prior from\" is not used."}'),
  ('ENG-0625', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Fixed prepositions', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.3', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
The patient has been suffering ______ fever for three days.', '["with", "from", "by", "of"]', 1, '"Suffer from" is used for illnesses.', '{"0": "\"Suffering with\" is not the standard form.", "2": "\"By\" does not follow \"suffer\" here.", "3": "\"Suffer of\" is not used."}'),
  ('ENG-0626', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Fixed prepositions', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.3', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
He was accused ______ cheating, and later charged ______ fraud.', '["with / of", "of / with", "for / with", "of / for"]', 1, 'The fixed forms are "accused of" and "charged with".', '{"0": "The prepositions are reversed.", "2": "\"Accused for\" is wrong.", "3": "\"Charged for\" means asked to pay a price, not formally accused."}'),
  ('ENG-0627', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Fixed prepositions', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.3', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
The patient was told to refrain ______ eating for eight hours before the test.', '["to", "of", "with", "from"]', 3, '"Refrain from" is the fixed combination, followed by a gerund.', '{"0": "\"Refrain to\" is a common error.", "1": "\"Refrain of\" is not used.", "2": "\"Refrain with\" is not used."}'),
  ('ENG-0628', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Prepositions of place and time', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.3', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
We arrived ______ Lahore ______ midnight.', '["in / at", "at / in", "to / at", "in / on"]', 0, '"Arrive in" is used for cities and countries; "at" is used for exact times such as midnight.', '{"1": "\"Arrive at\" is for smaller places such as a station, and \"in midnight\" is wrong.", "2": "\"Arrive to\" is not used.", "3": "\"On midnight\" is wrong."}'),
  ('ENG-0629', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Fixed prepositions', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.3', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
She was angry ______ her brother ______ breaking her phone.', '["at / with", "on / for", "with / for", "to / about"]', 2, 'We are angry "with" a person and "for" (or "about") what they did.', '{"0": "\"Angry with breaking\" is wrong.", "1": "\"Angry on\" is not standard.", "3": "\"Angry to\" is not used."}'),
  ('ENG-0630', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Between and among', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.3', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
The money was shared ______ the two sisters.', '["among", "with", "between", "in"]', 2, '"Between" is used for two people or things.', '{"0": "\"Among\" is used for three or more.", "1": "\"Shared with\" needs a different sentence: one sister shared it with the other.", "3": "\"Shared in\" does not fit."}'),
  ('ENG-0631', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Prepositions of movement', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.3', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
The cat jumped ______ the table and then hid ______ it.', '["onto / under", "into / on", "in / over", "at / below"]', 0, '"Onto" shows movement to a surface; "under" shows position below.', '{"1": "You do not jump \"into\" a table.", "2": "\"In the table\" is wrong, and \"hid over it\" makes no sense.", "3": "\"Jumped at the table\" means attacked it."}'),
  ('ENG-0632', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Prepositions of time', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.3', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
She was born ______ the morning of 14 August.', '["in", "at", "by", "on"]', 3, 'We say "in the morning" in general, but "on" when the morning is a particular dated day.', '{"0": "\"In the morning\" is general; a specific dated morning takes \"on\".", "1": "\"At the morning\" is wrong.", "2": "\"By\" means not later than."}'),
  ('ENG-0633', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Prepositions of movement', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.3', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
The train passed ______ a long tunnel.', '["across", "through", "over", "onto"]', 1, '"Through" is movement from one end of an enclosed space to the other.', '{"0": "\"Across\" is movement over a surface from one side to the other.", "2": "A train goes through a tunnel, not over it.", "3": "\"Onto\" means moving to a surface."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0634', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Fixed prepositions', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.3', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
She is fond ______ music and proud ______ her voice.', '["with / of", "of / of", "for / about", "of / with"]', 1, 'Both "fond of" and "proud of" are fixed combinations.', '{"0": "\"Fond with\" is wrong.", "2": "Neither is standard.", "3": "\"Proud with\" is wrong."}'),
  ('ENG-0635', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Fixed prepositions', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.3', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
The patient complained ______ pain ______ his chest.', '["for / at", "with / on", "to / at", "of / in"]', 3, '"Complain of" is used for symptoms, and pain is felt "in" a part of the body.', '{"0": "\"Complain for\" is wrong, and pain is \"in\" the chest.", "1": "Neither preposition fits.", "2": "\"Complain to\" is used for the person you complain to."}'),
  ('ENG-0636', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Fixed prepositions', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.3', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
The train is bound ______ Karachi.', '["for", "to", "at", "in"]', 0, '"Bound for" means travelling towards a place.', '{"1": "\"Bound to\" means certain to, as in \"bound to win\".", "2": "\"Bound at\" is not used.", "3": "\"Bound in\" means covered, as a book bound in leather."}'),
  ('ENG-0637', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Emphasis', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the best transition:
Smoking damages the lungs. ______, it is the leading cause of preventable lung cancer.', '["However", "Otherwise", "Meanwhile", "In fact"]', 3, '''In fact'' adds a stronger, emphatic point to the first statement.', '{"0": "However signals contrast, but the second point supports the first.", "1": "''Otherwise'' means ''if not'', which makes no sense here.", "2": "Meanwhile signals time, not emphasis."}'),
  ('ENG-0638', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Clarification', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the best transition:
The drug is contraindicated in pregnancy; ______, pregnant women must not take it.', '["in other words", "on the other hand", "nevertheless", "similarly"]', 0, '''In other words'' restates the first idea more plainly, which is exactly what the second clause does.', '{"1": "The second clause does not contrast with the first.", "2": "Nevertheless signals a surprising contrast.", "3": "Similarly introduces a parallel case, not a restatement."}'),
  ('ENG-0639', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Time', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the best transition:
Wash your hands thoroughly. ______, put on the sterile gloves.', '["Nevertheless", "Instead", "Afterwards", "Otherwise"]', 2, '''Afterwards'' shows the order of steps in time.', '{"0": "There is no contrast between the steps.", "1": "Instead would mean the gloves replace hand-washing.", "3": "''Otherwise'' means ''if you do not'', which does not fit."}'),
  ('ENG-0640', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Concession', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the best transition:
______, the new hospital is far from the city; still, its facilities are excellent.', '["Admittedly", "Consequently", "Similarly", "For example"]', 0, '''Admittedly'' concedes a weak point before the writer''s main point (''still, its facilities are excellent'').', '{"1": "Consequently signals a result, but the distance is not a result of anything stated.", "2": "Similarly needs an earlier parallel idea.", "3": "There is no general statement for this to be an example of."}'),
  ('ENG-0641', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Emphasis', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Which transition signals emphasis?', '["in contrast", "as a result", "for example", "above all"]', 3, '''Above all'' marks the most important point.', '{"0": "In contrast signals difference.", "1": "As a result signals effect.", "2": "For example introduces an illustration."}'),
  ('ENG-0642', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Time', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the best transition:
The nurse checked the drip. ______, the doctor reviewed the chart at the desk.', '["Therefore", "Meanwhile", "Instead", "Otherwise"]', 1, '''Meanwhile'' shows two actions happening at the same time.', '{"0": "The doctor reviewing the chart is not a result of the drip check.", "2": "Instead suggests one action replaced the other.", "3": "''Otherwise'' means ''if not'', which does not fit."}'),
  ('ENG-0643', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Summary', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the best transition:
The drug is cheap, safe and effective. ______, it is the best choice for rural clinics.', '["For instance", "In contrast", "All in all", "Meanwhile"]', 2, '''All in all'' sums up the points just made.', '{"0": "The second sentence is a conclusion, not an example.", "1": "There is no contrast.", "3": "Meanwhile signals time."}'),
  ('ENG-0644', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Example', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the best transition:
Many fruits are rich in vitamin C; oranges and guavas, ______, contain large amounts.', '["for instance", "however", "therefore", "otherwise"]', 0, 'Oranges and guavas are examples of fruits rich in vitamin C.', '{"1": "There is no contrast.", "2": "The second clause is not a result.", "3": "''Otherwise'' means ''if not''."}'),
  ('ENG-0645', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Cause and result', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
The trial was very small; ______, its results should be treated with caution.', '["consequently", "however", "moreover", "nevertheless"]', 0, 'The caution is a result of the small size, so a result transition is needed.', '{"1": "There is no contrast.", "2": "\"Moreover\" adds a further point; it does not show result.", "3": "\"Nevertheless\" signals a surprising contrast."}'),
  ('ENG-0646', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Addition', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
The drug is effective. ______, it is cheap, which makes it ideal for rural clinics.', '["Nevertheless", "Otherwise", "Moreover", "Instead"]', 2, 'A second positive point is added, so an addition transition is needed.', '{"0": "There is no contrast between the two positive points.", "1": "\"Otherwise\" means \"if not\".", "3": "\"Instead\" signals replacement."}'),
  ('ENG-0647', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Condition', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
Take the tablets with food; ______, they may upset your stomach.', '["however", "moreover", "otherwise", "similarly"]', 2, '"Otherwise" means "if you do not do this".', '{"0": "The second clause is not simply a contrast.", "1": "It is not an additional point.", "3": "There is no parallel case."}'),
  ('ENG-0648', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Contrast', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
______ it rained heavily, the match continued.', '["Despite", "In spite of", "However", "Although"]', 3, '"Although" is a conjunction and introduces a full clause ("it rained heavily").', '{"0": "\"Despite\" must be followed by a noun phrase, not a clause.", "1": "\"In spite of\" also needs a noun phrase.", "2": "\"However\" does not join a dependent clause to a main clause like this."}'),
  ('ENG-0649', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Contrast', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
Some patients recover within days, ______ others take several weeks.', '["therefore", "whereas", "so that", "because"]', 1, '"Whereas" joins two contrasting facts in one sentence.', '{"0": "There is no cause and effect.", "2": "\"So that\" expresses purpose.", "3": "There is no cause."}'),
  ('ENG-0650', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Purpose', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
He saved his pocket money ______ buy a microscope.', '["so that", "in order that", "for", "in order to"]', 3, '"In order to" + base verb expresses purpose.', '{"0": "\"So that\" must be followed by a clause with a subject, such as \"so that he could buy\".", "1": "\"In order that\" must also be followed by a clause.", "2": "\"For\" cannot be followed by a base verb."}'),
  ('ENG-0651', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Concession', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
Granted, the method is slow; ______, it is very accurate.', '["still", "therefore", "for example", "similarly"]', 0, '"Granted... still" concedes a weakness and then states the main point.', '{"1": "Accuracy is not a result of slowness.", "2": "There is no example.", "3": "There is no parallel."}'),
  ('ENG-0652', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Cause and result', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
Vaccination rates rose sharply. ______, measles cases fell to a record low.', '["In contrast", "Nevertheless", "As a result", "For instance"]', 2, 'The fall in cases is the effect of higher vaccination.', '{"0": "\"Rose\" and \"fell\" may look opposite, but the relation is cause and effect.", "1": "Nothing unexpected follows.", "3": "The second sentence is not an example."}'),
  ('ENG-0653', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Cause and result', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
The patient is allergic to penicillin; ______, the doctor prescribed a different antibiotic.', '["nonetheless", "in contrast", "accordingly", "similarly"]', 2, '"Accordingly" means "for that reason", so it shows the result of the allergy.', '{"0": "\"Nonetheless\" signals contrast.", "1": "There is no contrast.", "3": "There is no parallel."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0654', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Contrast', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9, 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["The test was hard; however, everyone passed.", "The test was hard, however everyone passed.", "The test was hard however, everyone passed.", "The test was hard; however everyone, passed."]', 0, 'When "however" joins two independent clauses, a semicolon goes before it and a comma after it.', '{"1": "This is a comma splice.", "2": "A semicolon or full stop is needed before \"however\".", "3": "The comma is in the wrong place."}'),
  ('ENG-0655', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Contrast', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
______ his injury, he completed the race.', '["Although", "However", "In spite of", "Even though"]', 2, '"In spite of" is followed by a noun phrase ("his injury").', '{"0": "\"Although\" needs a clause with a verb.", "1": "\"However\" cannot take a noun as its object.", "3": "\"Even though\" needs a clause with a verb."}'),
  ('ENG-0656', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Contrast', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
The new drug is expensive; ______, it is the only effective option, so hospitals buy it.', '["therefore", "for instance", "nonetheless", "likewise"]', 2, 'The second clause holds true despite the first, so a concession/contrast word is needed.', '{"0": "Being the only option is not a result of being expensive.", "1": "There is no example.", "3": "There is no similarity."}'),
  ('ENG-0657', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Dash', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Which sentence uses the dash correctly?', '["She had — one goal to become a surgeon.", "She — had one goal, to become a surgeon.", "She had one — goal to become a surgeon.", "She had one goal — to become a surgeon."]', 3, 'A dash can introduce an explanation or emphasis at the end of a sentence: here it introduces what the goal was.', '{"0": "The dash splits the verb from its object for no reason.", "1": "The dash separates the subject from its verb.", "2": "The dash splits ''one goal''."}'),
  ('ENG-0658', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Brackets', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Which sentence uses brackets correctly?', '["The World Health Organization (WHO) issued new guidelines.", "The World Health (Organization WHO) issued new guidelines.", "(The World Health Organization WHO) issued new guidelines.", "The World Health Organization WHO (issued) new guidelines."]', 0, 'Brackets (parentheses) enclose extra information, such as an abbreviation given after the full name.', '{"1": "The brackets break the name apart.", "2": "The subject itself should not be in brackets, and the abbreviation is not separated.", "3": "The verb is not extra information."}'),
  ('ENG-0659', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Abbreviations', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Which abbreviation means "that is to say"?', '["e.g.", "etc.", "i.e.", "et al."]', 2, '''i.e.'' (Latin id est) means ''that is''; it explains or restates.', '{"0": "''e.g.'' means ''for example''.", "1": "''etc.'' means ''and so on''.", "3": "''et al.'' means ''and others'', used for authors."}'),
  ('ENG-0660', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Abbreviations', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["Bring pens, pencils, erasers, etc..", "Bring pens, pencils, erasers, etc", "Bring pens, pencils, erasers, etc.", "Bring, pens, pencils, erasers, etc."]', 2, 'When a sentence ends with an abbreviation such as ''etc.'', its full stop also ends the sentence; no second full stop is added.', '{"0": "A double full stop is never used.", "1": "The abbreviation and the sentence both need the full stop.", "3": "No comma comes between a verb and its object."}'),
  ('ENG-0661', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Ellipsis', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'In "I was going to tell you, but ... never mind," the three dots show that:', '["the speaker trails off or leaves something out", "a list follows", "a quotation begins", "something belongs to someone"]', 0, 'An ellipsis (...) marks words left out or a sentence that trails off.', '{"1": "A list would follow a colon.", "2": "Quotation marks show a quotation.", "3": "Possession is shown with an apostrophe."}'),
  ('ENG-0662', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Capital letters', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Which sentence uses capital letters correctly?', '["Last ramadan, my Aunt visited the badshahi mosque in Lahore.", "Last Ramadan, my Aunt visited the Badshahi mosque in lahore.", "last Ramadan, my aunt visited the Badshahi Mosque in Lahore.", "Last Ramadan, my aunt visited the Badshahi Mosque in Lahore."]', 3, 'Months and religious occasions (Ramadan), names of particular buildings (Badshahi Mosque) and places (Lahore) take capitals; ''aunt'' after ''my'' is a common noun.', '{"0": "''Ramadan'' and ''Badshahi Mosque'' need capitals; ''aunt'' after ''my'' does not.", "1": "''Lahore'' and ''Mosque'' (part of the name) need capitals; ''aunt'' does not.", "2": "The first word of a sentence needs a capital."}'),
  ('ENG-0663', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Capital letters', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Which sentence uses capital letters correctly?', '["We drove North to swat in the Summer.", "We drove north to Swat in the summer.", "We drove north to Swat in the Summer.", "we drove north to Swat in the summer."]', 1, 'Directions (north) and seasons (summer) are common nouns; place names (Swat) are capitalised.', '{"0": "''North'' and ''Summer'' should be lower case and ''Swat'' capitalised.", "2": "Seasons are not capitalised.", "3": "The sentence must begin with a capital."}'),
  ('ENG-0664', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Capital letters', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Which book title is capitalised correctly?', '["The old man and the sea", "The Old Man and the Sea", "The Old Man And The Sea", "the Old Man and the Sea"]', 1, 'In titles, the first word and the main words take capitals; short articles and conjunctions (''and'', ''the'') in the middle stay lower case.', '{"0": "Main words in a title need capitals.", "2": "''and'' and ''the'' in the middle of a title stay lower case.", "3": "The first word of a title is always capitalised."}'),
  ('ENG-0665', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Capital letters', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Which sentence uses capital letters correctly?', '["She speaks english and is studying Chemistry.", "She speaks english and is studying chemistry.", "She Speaks English and is studying chemistry.", "She speaks English and is studying chemistry."]', 3, 'Languages (English) are proper nouns and are capitalised; ordinary school subjects such as chemistry are common nouns.', '{"0": "''English'' needs a capital and ''chemistry'' does not.", "1": "''English'' is a language and needs a capital.", "2": "''Speaks'' is an ordinary verb and needs no capital."}'),
  ('ENG-0666', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Apostrophe', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
The ______ hostel is next to the library.', '["womens''", "womens", "womans''", "women''s"]', 3, '"Women" is already plural, so the possessive adds ''s: women''s.', '{"0": "\"Womens\" is not a word.", "1": "The possessive needs an apostrophe.", "2": "This form does not exist."}'),
  ('ENG-0667', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Apostrophe', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'The car belongs to the Khan family (the Khans).
Choose the correct form:
The ______ car is parked outside.', '["Khan''s", "Khans''", "Khans", "Khans''s"]', 1, 'For a plural ending in -s, add only an apostrophe: the Khans'' car.', '{"0": "This means one person named Khan.", "2": "The possessive needs an apostrophe.", "3": "A plural ending in -s takes only the apostrophe."}'),
  ('ENG-0668', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Apostrophe', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
The hospital updated ______ website; ______ now much easier to use.', '["it''s / its", "its / it''s", "its / its", "it''s / it''s"]', 1, '"Its" is the possessive; "it''s" means "it is".', '{"0": "They are reversed.", "2": "The second blank needs \"it is\".", "3": "The first blank needs the possessive."}'),
  ('ENG-0669', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Semicolon', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["Delegates came from Lahore, Punjab, Quetta, Balochistan, and Peshawar, Khyber Pakhtunkhwa.", "Delegates came from: Lahore; Punjab, Quetta; Balochistan, and Peshawar; Khyber Pakhtunkhwa.", "Delegates came from Lahore Punjab; Quetta Balochistan; and Peshawar Khyber Pakhtunkhwa.", "Delegates came from Lahore, Punjab; Quetta, Balochistan; and Peshawar, Khyber Pakhtunkhwa."]', 3, 'When items in a list already contain commas, semicolons separate the items.', '{"0": "With only commas, it is unclear which city goes with which province.", "1": "The semicolons split each city from its province.", "2": "Each city and province still needs a comma between them."}'),
  ('ENG-0670', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Comma', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Which sentence means that ONLY the students who cheat will be expelled?', '["Students, who cheat, will be expelled.", "Students who, cheat will be expelled.", "Students who cheat will be expelled.", "Students, who cheat will be expelled."]', 2, 'Without commas the clause is defining: it picks out which students.', '{"0": "With commas the clause is extra information, implying that all students cheat.", "1": "The comma is misplaced.", "3": "A single comma breaks the subject from its clause."}'),
  ('ENG-0671', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Quotation marks', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["She asked, \"Is the clinic open\"?", "She asked \"Is the clinic open?\".", "She asked, \"Is the clinic open?\"", "She asked, \"is the clinic open?\""]', 2, 'The question mark belongs to the quoted question, so it goes inside the closing quotation marks; no extra full stop is added.', '{"0": "The quoted words are the question, so the mark goes inside.", "1": "A comma is needed before the quote and no full stop after.", "3": "A quoted sentence starts with a capital letter."}'),
  ('ENG-0672', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Hyphen', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Which phrase is hyphenated correctly?', '["a five-year-old child", "a five year-old child", "a five-years-old child", "a five year old-child"]', 0, 'A compound adjective before a noun is joined with hyphens, and "year" stays singular.', '{"1": "All three words need joining.", "2": "\"Year\" is singular in a compound adjective.", "3": "The hyphen joins the wrong words."}'),
  ('ENG-0673', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Comma', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["I wanted to come but, I was ill.", "I wanted to come, but I was ill.", "I wanted, to come but I was ill.", "I wanted to come; but, I was ill."]', 1, 'A comma goes before a coordinating conjunction ("but") that joins two independent clauses.', '{"0": "The comma goes before \"but\", not after it.", "2": "The comma splits the verb from its object.", "3": "This adds unneeded marks around \"but\"."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0674', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Dash', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["The results, though delayed — were worth the wait.", "The results — though delayed, were worth the wait.", "The results though — delayed were worth the wait.", "The results — though delayed — were worth the wait."]', 3, 'An interruption is enclosed by a matching pair of dashes (or a pair of commas).', '{"0": "A comma and a dash do not make a matching pair.", "1": "The pair is mismatched.", "2": "The dash splits the phrase."}'),
  ('ENG-0675', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Capital letters', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Which sentence uses capital letters correctly?', '["The principal met Professor Rahman at the Faisal Mosque on Friday.", "The principal met professor Rahman at the Faisal mosque on Friday.", "The principal met Professor Rahman at the Faisal Mosque on friday.", "The Principal Met Professor Rahman at the Faisal Mosque on Friday."]', 0, 'A title placed before a name (Professor Rahman), the name of a particular building (Faisal Mosque) and days of the week are capitalised; "principal" used as an ordinary noun is not.', '{"1": "\"Professor\" before a name and \"Mosque\" as part of a name need capitals.", "2": "Days of the week need capitals.", "3": "\"Met\" is an ordinary verb, and \"principal\" here is a common noun."}'),
  ('ENG-0676', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Colon', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Which sentence uses the colon correctly?', '["The result was clear: the drug did not work.", "The result was: clear the drug did not work.", "The: result was clear, the drug did not work.", "The result: was clear the drug did not work."]', 0, 'A colon can follow a complete clause to introduce an explanation of it.', '{"1": "The colon splits the verb from its complement.", "2": "A colon cannot come after \"The\".", "3": "The colon splits the subject from its verb."}'),
  ('ENG-0677', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Hyphen', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
The old sofa was torn, so they had it ______ in new fabric.', '["recovered", "recover-ed", "re covered", "re-covered"]', 3, 'A hyphen separates the prefix to avoid confusion with a different word: re-cover (cover again) versus recover (get better or get back).', '{"0": "\"Recovered\" means got better or got back, which changes the meaning.", "1": "A hyphen is never placed before an ending.", "2": "The prefix is joined with a hyphen, not a space."}'),
  ('ENG-0678', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Comma', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.3', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["Doctor is the patient awake?", "Doctor, is the patient awake?", "Doctor, is the patient awake.", "Doctor is, the patient awake?"]', 1, 'A person addressed directly is set off with a comma, and a direct question ends with a question mark.', '{"0": "The comma after the name of the person addressed is missing.", "2": "A direct question needs a question mark.", "3": "The comma is in the wrong place."}'),
  ('ENG-0679', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Sentence fragments', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.2', 'Which of the following is a complete sentence?', '["Although the results were promising.", "Because more trials are needed.", "The results, which were promising.", "Although the results were promising, more trials are needed."]', 3, 'It has an independent clause (''more trials are needed'') as well as the dependent ''although'' clause.', '{"0": "An ''although'' clause cannot stand alone.", "1": "A ''because'' clause cannot stand alone.", "2": "There is a subject but no main verb."}'),
  ('ENG-0680', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Kinds of sentences', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'What kind of sentence is this?
"How quickly the wound has healed!"', '["interrogative", "exclamatory", "imperative", "declarative"]', 1, 'It expresses strong feeling and ends with an exclamation mark.', '{"0": "Although it starts with ''How'', it does not ask a question; the subject comes before the verb.", "2": "It does not give a command.", "3": "It expresses strong feeling rather than a plain statement."}'),
  ('ENG-0681', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Kinds of sentences', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'What kind of sentence is this?
"Please hand me the forceps."', '["declarative", "imperative", "interrogative", "exclamatory"]', 1, 'It gives a request or command, with the subject (you) understood.', '{"0": "It does not simply state a fact.", "2": "It does not ask a question.", "3": "It does not express strong feeling."}'),
  ('ENG-0682', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Kinds of sentences', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Which sentence is interrogative?', '["Tell me if the report has arrived.", "I wonder whether the report has arrived.", "What a long report this is!", "Has the report arrived yet?"]', 3, 'An interrogative sentence asks a direct question and ends with a question mark.', '{"0": "This is an imperative (a command), though it seeks information.", "1": "This is a statement (declarative) about wondering.", "2": "This is exclamatory."}'),
  ('ENG-0683', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Phrases and clauses', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Identify the participial phrase in:
"Exhausted by the long shift, the nurse fell asleep."', '["Exhausted by the long shift", "the nurse", "fell asleep", "the long shift"]', 0, 'It begins with a past participle (''exhausted'') and describes the nurse.', '{"1": "This is the subject, a noun phrase.", "2": "This is the verb and its complement.", "3": "This is a noun phrase inside the participial phrase."}'),
  ('ENG-0684', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Phrases and clauses', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'In "The exam over, the students went home," the words "The exam over" form:', '["a noun clause", "an adverb clause", "an absolute phrase", "an infinitive phrase"]', 2, 'An absolute phrase is a noun plus a modifier (here "the exam" + "over") with no finite verb; it modifies the whole sentence.', '{"0": "A clause needs a finite verb; there is none.", "1": "There is no verb and no subordinating conjunction.", "3": "There is no ''to'' + verb."}'),
  ('ENG-0685', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Phrases and clauses', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'In "Dr Ayesha, our new cardiologist, joined today," the words "our new cardiologist" are:', '["an adjective clause", "a participial phrase", "an appositive phrase", "an adverb phrase"]', 2, 'An appositive is a noun phrase placed next to another noun to rename it.', '{"0": "There is no verb, so it is not a clause.", "1": "There is no participle.", "3": "It renames a noun; it does not modify a verb."}'),
  ('ENG-0686', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Misplaced modifiers', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Which sentence makes it clear that the DOCTOR, not the patient, was wearing a white coat?', '["The doctor examined the patient wearing a white coat.", "The doctor examined the patient, who was wearing a white coat.", "The patient wearing a white coat was examined by the doctor.", "Wearing a white coat, the doctor examined the patient."]', 3, 'The opening participial phrase sits right before "the doctor", so it clearly describes the doctor.', '{"0": "The phrase is next to \"the patient\", so it can be read as describing the patient.", "1": "This clearly says the patient wore the coat.", "2": "The coat belongs to the patient here."}'),
  ('ENG-0687', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Phrases and clauses', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'In "She placed the slide under the microscope," the phrase "under the microscope" works as:', '["an adjective", "an adverb", "a noun", "a verb"]', 1, 'It tells where she placed the slide, so this prepositional phrase modifies the verb.', '{"0": "It does not describe which slide.", "2": "It is not the subject or object.", "3": "It contains no verb."}'),
  ('ENG-0688', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Phrases and clauses', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'In "I will wait until you return," the words "until you return" form:', '["a noun clause", "an adjective clause", "an adverb clause of time", "a prepositional phrase"]', 2, 'It has a subject and verb and tells when I will wait.', '{"0": "It does not act as subject or object.", "1": "It does not describe a noun.", "3": "It has its own subject and verb, so it is a clause."}'),
  ('ENG-0689', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Phrases and clauses', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'In "The reason why he left is unclear," the words "why he left" form:', '["an adjective clause", "a noun clause", "an adverb clause", "an independent clause"]', 0, 'It describes the noun "reason" (which reason?).', '{"1": "It follows and describes a noun; it is not the subject itself.", "2": "It does not modify a verb.", "3": "It cannot stand alone."}'),
  ('ENG-0690', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Phrases and clauses', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'In "I know where he lives," the words "where he lives" form:', '["an adjective clause", "an adverb clause of place", "a prepositional phrase", "a noun clause"]', 3, 'It is the object of "know" (I know what? where he lives).', '{"0": "There is no noun before it to describe.", "1": "It answers \"what do I know?\", not \"where do I know?\".", "2": "It has a subject and verb."}'),
  ('ENG-0691', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Phrases and clauses', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'In "She went where the work was," the words "where the work was" form:', '["a noun clause", "an adverb clause of place", "an adjective clause", "a participial phrase"]', 1, 'It tells where she went, modifying the verb "went".', '{"0": "It is not the object of \"went\".", "2": "There is no noun before it to describe.", "3": "It has a finite verb."}'),
  ('ENG-0692', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Kinds of sentences', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', '"Although she was tired, she finished the report, and then she went to bed."
This sentence is:', '["complex", "compound-complex", "compound", "simple"]', 1, 'It has two independent clauses joined by "and" plus one dependent clause ("Although she was tired").', '{"0": "A complex sentence has only one independent clause.", "2": "A compound sentence has no dependent clause.", "3": "A simple sentence has one clause."}'),
  ('ENG-0693', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Phrases and clauses', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'In "The boy sitting near the window is my cousin," the words "sitting near the window" form:', '["a gerund phrase", "an adverb clause", "an infinitive phrase", "a participial phrase used as an adjective"]', 3, 'It begins with a present participle and describes "the boy".', '{"0": "A gerund phrase acts as a noun, but this one describes a noun.", "1": "It has no subject or finite verb.", "2": "There is no \"to\"."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0694', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Inversion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
Never before ______ such a rare case.', '["the doctors had seen", "had seen the doctors", "had the doctors seen", "seen had the doctors"]', 2, 'After a fronted negative such as "Never before", the auxiliary comes before the subject.', '{"0": "The word order must be inverted.", "1": "This makes \"the doctors\" the object.", "3": "This word order is wrong."}'),
  ('ENG-0695', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Inversion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
So severe ______ that the school was closed.', '["was the storm", "the storm was", "did the storm", "the storm is"]', 0, 'After "So + adjective" at the front, the verb comes before the subject.', '{"1": "Inversion is needed.", "2": "\"Did\" is not used with \"be\".", "3": "The tense and word order are wrong."}'),
  ('ENG-0696', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Inversion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
Little ______ that his life was about to change.', '["he knew", "he did know", "knew he", "did he know"]', 3, 'After a fronted negative "Little", the auxiliary "did" comes before the subject.', '{"0": "Inversion is needed.", "1": "The auxiliary must come before the subject.", "2": "\"Did\" is used, not the main verb."}'),
  ('ENG-0697', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Inversion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
Rarely ______ a patient recover so quickly.', '["do", "does", "is", "are"]', 1, 'After "Rarely", "does" comes before the singular subject "a patient", followed by the base verb.', '{"0": "\"Do\" does not agree with the singular \"a patient\".", "2": "\"Is\" cannot be followed by the base verb \"recover\".", "3": "\"Are\" does not agree and cannot be followed by \"recover\"."}'),
  ('ENG-0698', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Inversion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
______ the warning earlier, they would have evacuated.', '["If they received", "Had they received", "They had received", "Have they received"]', 1, '"Had they received" is the inverted form of "If they had received" in a third conditional.', '{"0": "The result \"would have\" needs the past perfect.", "2": "This is a statement, not a condition.", "3": "The present perfect does not fit."}'),
  ('ENG-0699', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Phrases and clauses', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'In "The fact that he lied upset her," the words "that he lied" form:', '["an adjective clause", "a noun clause in apposition to \"the fact\"", "an adverb clause", "an independent clause"]', 1, 'The clause tells us what the fact is (it renames it); "that" is a conjunction here, not a relative pronoun.', '{"0": "In an adjective clause \"that\" would stand for something inside the clause; here nothing is missing from \"he lied\".", "2": "It does not modify a verb.", "3": "It cannot stand alone here."}'),
  ('ENG-0700', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Inversion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
Not until the patient woke up ______ the extent of his injuries.', '["the doctors understood", "understood the doctors", "the doctors did understand", "did the doctors understand"]', 3, 'After a fronted "Not until...", the main clause is inverted with "did".', '{"0": "Inversion is needed.", "1": "Main verbs are not inverted.", "2": "The auxiliary must come before the subject."}'),
  ('ENG-0701', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Inversion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11, 2.12; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 2.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
______ it not been for your help, I would have failed.', '["Had", "Were", "If", "Should"]', 0, '"Had it not been for..." is the inverted form of "If it had not been for..."', '{"1": "\"Were it not for\" is used without \"been\".", "2": "\"If it not been\" is not correct.", "3": "\"Should\" does not combine with \"been\" here."}'),
  ('ENG-0702', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Impersonal and causative passive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"People say that he is honest."', '["He is said being honest.", "He is said to be honest.", "It is said him to be honest.", "He said to be honest."]', 1, '''People say that he is...'' becomes ''He is said to be...'' (subject + passive reporting verb + to-infinitive).', '{"0": "''Said'' is followed by a to-infinitive, not -ing.", "2": "With ''It is said'', a that-clause follows: ''It is said that he is honest''.", "3": "The passive needs ''is'': ''He is said''."}'),
  ('ENG-0703', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Impersonal and causative passive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct sentence with the same meaning as:
"It is believed that the virus spread from bats."', '["The virus is believed to spread from bats.", "The virus is believed to have spread from bats.", "The virus is believed to be spread from bats.", "The virus is believed spreading from bats."]', 1, 'The spreading happened before the believing, so the perfect infinitive (to have spread) is needed.', '{"0": "The simple infinitive suggests a present, general fact, not a past event.", "2": "This is passive and present, which changes the meaning.", "3": "''Believed'' is followed by a to-infinitive."}'),
  ('ENG-0704', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Impersonal and causative passive', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the sentence that correctly uses the causative form:
(A mechanic repaired my car because I asked him to.)', '["I had my car repair.", "I had repaired my car.", "I was had my car repaired.", "I had my car repaired."]', 3, 'The causative ''have + object + past participle'' shows that someone else did the job for you.', '{"0": "The past participle (repaired) is needed.", "1": "This past perfect means I repaired it myself.", "2": "This is not a correct verb form."}'),
  ('ENG-0705', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Impersonal and causative passive', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct form:
She got her blood pressure ______ at the clinic.', '["checked", "check", "checking", "to check"]', 0, '''Get + object + past participle'' is the causative: someone checked it for her.', '{"1": "The past participle is needed after the object.", "2": "The -ing form would mean the blood pressure was doing the checking.", "3": "''Get something to check'' would make the blood pressure the one checking."}'),
  ('ENG-0706', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Active to passive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"They are repairing the road."', '["The road is being repaired.", "The road is repaired.", "The road has been repairing.", "The road is been repaired."]', 0, 'Present continuous passive: is/are + being + past participle.', '{"1": "This loses the continuous meaning.", "2": "This is not a passive form.", "3": "\"Is been\" is not a verb form."}'),
  ('ENG-0707', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Imperatives in the passive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"Let him close the door."', '["Let the door is closed by him.", "Let the door closed by him.", "Let the door be closed by him.", "The door let be closed by him."]', 2, 'With "let", the passive is "let + object + be + past participle".', '{"0": "\"Let\" is followed by the base form \"be\".", "1": "\"Be\" is missing.", "3": "The word order is wrong."}'),
  ('ENG-0708', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Modals in the passive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"They should have informed us."', '["We should have been informed.", "We should be informed.", "We should have informed.", "We should had been informed."]', 0, 'Perfect modal passive: modal + have been + past participle.', '{"1": "This loses the perfect (past) meaning.", "2": "This is active.", "3": "A modal is followed by \"have\", not \"had\"."}'),
  ('ENG-0709', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Passive to active', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct active form of:
"She was laughed at by the class."', '["The class laughed her.", "The class was laughing at she.", "She laughed at the class.", "The class laughed at her."]', 3, 'With a prepositional verb ("laugh at"), the preposition stays with the verb.', '{"0": "The preposition \"at\" is lost.", "1": "The tense changes and \"she\" should be \"her\".", "2": "The roles are reversed."}'),
  ('ENG-0710', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Active to passive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"They elected him captain."', '["Captain was elected him.", "He was elected captain.", "He elected captain.", "He was elected to captain."]', 1, 'The object (him) becomes the subject; the complement (captain) stays after the verb.', '{"0": "The complement cannot become the subject.", "2": "This is active and means he did the electing.", "3": "\"To\" is not used with this complement."}'),
  ('ENG-0711', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Active to passive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"It is time to close the shop."', '["It is time to be closed the shop.", "It is time for the shop being closed.", "It is time the shop to be closed.", "It is time for the shop to be closed."]', 3, 'The infinitive becomes a passive infinitive (to be + past participle), with "for" introducing its subject.', '{"0": "The word order is wrong.", "1": "A to-infinitive is needed.", "2": "\"For\" is needed before \"the shop\"."}'),
  ('ENG-0712', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Passive to active', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct active form of:
"The thief was caught red-handed."', '["Someone caught the thief red-handed.", "The thief caught red-handed.", "Someone was catching the thief red-handed.", "Someone had caught the thief red-handed."]', 0, 'When no agent is given, an active form uses a general subject such as "someone" and keeps the tense.', '{"1": "This drops the passive but makes the thief the catcher.", "2": "The tense changes to past continuous.", "3": "The tense changes to past perfect."}'),
  ('ENG-0713', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Questions in the passive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"Why did he punish you?"', '["Why was you punished by him?", "Why did you punished by him?", "Why were you punished by him?", "Why you were punished by him?"]', 2, 'Past simple question: Why + were/was + subject + past participle. "You" takes "were".', '{"0": "\"You\" takes \"were\".", "1": "\"Did\" is not used in the passive.", "3": "A question needs the auxiliary before the subject."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0714', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Active to passive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"I saw him leave the building."', '["He was seen leave the building.", "He was saw to leave the building.", "He was seen to leave the building.", "He seen to leave the building."]', 2, 'A bare infinitive after "see" in the active becomes a to-infinitive in the passive.', '{"0": "\"To\" is needed in the passive.", "1": "The past participle is \"seen\".", "3": "The auxiliary \"was\" is missing."}'),
  ('ENG-0715', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Active to passive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"The doctor is going to discharge the patient."', '["The patient is going to be discharged by the doctor.", "The patient is going to discharged by the doctor.", "The patient is being discharged by the doctor.", "The patient is going to be discharge by the doctor."]', 0, '"Going to" stays, and the infinitive becomes passive: going to be + past participle.', '{"1": "\"Be\" is missing.", "2": "The future meaning of \"going to\" is lost.", "3": "The past participle is needed."}'),
  ('ENG-0716', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Active to passive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"Somebody is following us."', '["We are followed.", "We are being follow.", "We are being followed.", "We have been following."]', 2, 'Present continuous passive: am/is/are + being + past participle.', '{"0": "The continuous meaning is lost.", "1": "The past participle is needed.", "3": "This is active."}'),
  ('ENG-0717', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Imperatives in the passive', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"Please help me."', '["You are requested help me.", "Me be helped please.", "You are requested to help me.", "You were requested to help me."]', 2, 'A polite request is put into the passive with "You are requested to...".', '{"0": "\"To\" is needed.", "1": "This is not English.", "3": "The tense changes to the past."}'),
  ('ENG-0718', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Recognising voice', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Which sentence CANNOT normally be put into the passive voice?', '["She has a new laptop.", "She bought a new laptop.", "She repaired the laptop.", "She sold the laptop."]', 0, '"Have" meaning possess is a state verb that is not used in the passive.', '{"1": "\"A new laptop was bought by her\" is possible.", "2": "\"The laptop was repaired by her\" is possible.", "3": "\"The laptop was sold by her\" is possible."}'),
  ('ENG-0719', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Wishes and prayers', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
The old man said to me, "May you live long!"', '["The old man said to me that may I live long.", "The old man prayed that may you live long.", "The old man prayed that I might live long.", "The old man told me to live long."]', 2, 'Prayers and wishes with ''May...!'' use a reporting verb such as ''prayed'' or ''wished'', and ''may'' becomes ''might''.', '{"0": "The exclamatory word order is kept, which is wrong in reported speech.", "1": "The pronoun and word order are not changed.", "3": "A prayer is not a command."}'),
  ('ENG-0720', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Statements', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
He said, "I may come tomorrow."', '["He said that he may come tomorrow.", "He said that he might come tomorrow.", "He said that I might come the next day.", "He said that he might come the next day."]', 3, '"May" changes to "might" and "tomorrow" to "the next day" after a past reporting verb.', '{"0": "Neither the modal nor the time word is changed.", "1": "\"Tomorrow\" should become \"the next day\".", "2": "The pronoun should be \"he\"."}'),
  ('ENG-0721', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Questions', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
She said to me, "Have you finished your assignment?"', '["She asked me have I finished my assignment.", "She asked me if I had finished my assignment.", "She asked me if I have finished my assignment.", "She said to me that had I finished my assignment."]', 1, 'Yes/no questions use "if" or "whether"; the present perfect shifts back to past perfect; question word order is dropped.', '{"0": "Question word order and tense are kept.", "2": "The tense is not shifted.", "3": "Questions are reported with \"asked\", and the word order is wrong."}'),
  ('ENG-0722', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Commands and requests', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
The doctor said to me, "Do not eat sweets."', '["The doctor forbade me not to eat sweets.", "The doctor told me to not must eat sweets.", "The doctor said me that I do not eat sweets.", "The doctor forbade me to eat sweets."]', 3, '"Forbid" already contains the negative, so the infinitive is positive.', '{"0": "This creates a double negative.", "1": "\"Must\" cannot follow \"to\".", "2": "\"Said me\" is wrong, and the tense does not shift."}'),
  ('ENG-0723', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Statements', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
She said, "I was reading when the lights went out."', '["She said that she has been reading when the lights went out.", "She said that she had been reading when the lights go out.", "She said that she had been reading when the lights went out.", "She said that she is reading when the lights went out."]', 2, 'The past continuous main verb becomes past perfect continuous; the past simple in the time clause can stay as it is.', '{"0": "The present perfect continuous is not a backshift of the past.", "1": "The time clause cannot shift to the present.", "3": "The tense is not shifted back."}'),
  ('ENG-0724', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Commands and requests', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
He said to me, "Please wait here."', '["He requested me to wait here.", "He requested me that wait there.", "He requested me to wait there.", "He said me please wait there."]', 2, '"Please" becomes "requested"; the command becomes a to-infinitive; "here" becomes "there".', '{"0": "\"Here\" should become \"there\".", "1": "\"That\" is not used before an infinitive command.", "3": "\"Said me\" is wrong and \"please\" is not kept."}'),
  ('ENG-0725', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Statements', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
She said, "I will finish this work today."', '["She said that she would finish that work that day.", "She said that she would finish this work today.", "She said that she will finish that work that day.", "She said that she would finish that work today."]', 0, '"Will" becomes "would", "this" becomes "that", and "today" becomes "that day".', '{"1": "\"This\" and \"today\" are not changed.", "2": "\"Will\" is not changed to \"would\".", "3": "\"Today\" should become \"that day\"."}'),
  ('ENG-0726', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Indirect to direct', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct direct form of:
He told me that he had seen the film the day before.', '["He said to me, \"I had seen the film the day before.\"", "He said to me, \"He saw the film yesterday.\"", "He said to me, \"I see the film tomorrow.\"", "He said to me, \"I saw the film yesterday.\""]', 3, '"The day before" becomes "yesterday", "he" becomes "I", and the past perfect becomes the past simple.', '{"0": "The words were not changed back to direct speech.", "1": "The pronoun should be \"I\".", "2": "The tense and the time word are wrong."}'),
  ('ENG-0727', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Statements', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
The teacher said, "Water boils at 100 degrees Celsius at sea level."', '["The teacher said that water boiled at 100 degrees Celsius at sea level.", "The teacher said that water boils at 100 degrees Celsius at sea level.", "The teacher said that water had boiled at 100 degrees Celsius at sea level.", "The teacher said that water would boil at 100 degrees Celsius at sea level."]', 1, 'A universal truth keeps the present tense even after a past reporting verb.', '{"0": "A universal truth does not shift back.", "2": "This turns a general fact into a past event.", "3": "\"Would\" changes the meaning."}'),
  ('ENG-0728', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Exclamations', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
She said, "How beautiful the garden is!"', '["She exclaimed how beautiful was the garden.", "She asked how beautiful the garden was.", "She exclaimed that how beautiful the garden is.", "She exclaimed that the garden was very beautiful."]', 3, 'An exclamation is reported with "exclaimed" and a statement, with "how" replaced by "very" and the tense shifted.', '{"0": "The word order is wrong.", "1": "It is not a question.", "2": "\"That\" and \"how\" cannot both introduce the clause, and the tense is not shifted."}'),
  ('ENG-0729', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Questions', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
He said to me, "Do you know where the station is?"', '["He asked me if I knew where the station was.", "He asked me do I know where the station is.", "He asked me if I knew where was the station.", "He asked me if I know where the station was."]', 0, 'Both clauses shift back, and both use statement word order.', '{"1": "The question form and present tense are kept.", "2": "The embedded clause needs statement order.", "3": "\"Know\" should shift to \"knew\"."}'),
  ('ENG-0730', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Questions', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
He said to her, "What are you doing?"', '["He asked her what was she doing.", "He asked her what she is doing.", "He asked her that what she was doing.", "He asked her what she was doing."]', 3, 'A wh-question uses statement word order, and the present continuous shifts to past continuous.', '{"0": "Statement order is needed.", "1": "The tense is not shifted.", "2": "\"That\" is not used with a reported wh-question."}'),
  ('ENG-0731', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Exclamations', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
She said, "Hurrah! We have won the match."', '["She exclaimed with sorrow that they had won the match.", "She exclaimed with joy that they had won the match.", "She exclaimed hurrah that they had won the match.", "She exclaimed with joy that we had won the match."]', 1, '"Hurrah" is replaced by "with joy"; "we" becomes "they"; present perfect becomes past perfect.', '{"0": "\"Hurrah\" expresses joy.", "2": "The interjection is not kept.", "3": "\"We\" should become \"they\"."}'),
  ('ENG-0732', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Commands and requests', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 3.1', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
He said, "Let me go home early."', '["He said let me go home early.", "He requested that he might be allowed to go home early.", "He ordered that he should go home early.", "He requested that let him go home early."]', 1, '"Let me" asking permission is reported with "requested that he might be allowed to...".', '{"0": "The direct words are kept.", "2": "A request for permission is not an order.", "3": "\"Let\" cannot follow \"that\" like this."}'),
  ('ENG-0733', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Neither...nor and either...or', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
Neither the patient nor his relatives ______ aware of the risk.', '["was", "is", "were", "has been"]', 2, 'With neither...nor, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, "relatives" (plural).', '{"0": "The nearer subject is plural.", "1": "The nearer subject is plural, and the tense should match the context.", "3": "The nearer subject is plural."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0734', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Indefinite pronouns', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
Each of the students ______ given a certificate.', '["was", "were", "are", "have been"]', 0, '"Each" is singular, so it takes a singular verb.', '{"1": "\"Students\" is inside the \"of\" phrase; the subject is \"each\".", "2": "\"Each\" needs a singular verb.", "3": "\"Each\" needs a singular verb."}'),
  ('ENG-0735', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Relative clauses', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
One of the reasons that ______ given ______ unconvincing.', '["was / was", "were / was", "were / were", "was / were"]', 1, 'In the relative clause "that" refers to "reasons" (plural): were given. The main verb agrees with "One": was unconvincing.', '{"0": "The relative clause refers to the plural \"reasons\".", "2": "The main subject is \"One\", which is singular.", "3": "Both agreements are reversed."}'),
  ('ENG-0736', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Relative clauses', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
She is one of those doctors who ______ never late.', '["is", "was", "has been", "are"]', 3, '"Who" refers to "doctors" (plural), so the verb in the relative clause is plural.', '{"0": "\"Who\" refers to \"doctors\", not to \"one\".", "1": "The tense and number are wrong.", "2": "\"Who\" refers to a plural noun."}'),
  ('ENG-0737', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Collective nouns', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
The committee ______ divided in their opinions.', '["are", "is", "has", "does"]', 0, 'When the members act as individuals (shown by "their opinions"), the collective noun takes a plural verb.', '{"1": "\"Their opinions\" shows the members are being thought of separately.", "2": "\"Has divided\" changes the meaning.", "3": "\"Does divided\" is not a verb form."}'),
  ('ENG-0738', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Amounts', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
Twenty percent of the population ______ vaccinated, and twenty percent of the patients ______ recovered.', '["are / has", "is / has", "are / have", "is / have"]', 3, 'With percentages, the verb agrees with the noun after "of": "population" is singular, "patients" is plural.', '{"0": "Both agreements are reversed.", "1": "\"Patients\" is plural.", "2": "\"Population\" is singular."}'),
  ('ENG-0739', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Nouns ending in -s', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
The pair of shoes ______ under the bed.', '["are", "were", "have been", "is"]', 3, 'The subject is "pair" (singular); "of shoes" does not change it.', '{"0": "\"Shoes\" is inside the \"of\" phrase; the subject is \"pair\".", "1": "The subject is singular, and the tense should be present.", "2": "The subject is singular."}'),
  ('ENG-0740', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Amounts', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
A lot of the water ______ wasted, and a lot of the people ______ unhappy.', '["was / were", "were / was", "was / was", "were / were"]', 0, 'After "a lot of", the verb agrees with the noun: "water" is uncountable (singular), "people" is plural.', '{"1": "Both agreements are reversed.", "2": "\"People\" is plural.", "3": "\"Water\" is uncountable and takes a singular verb."}'),
  ('ENG-0741', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Neither...nor and either...or', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
Either you or he ______ to blame.', '["are", "am", "is", "were"]', 2, 'With either...or, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, "he".', '{"0": "The nearer subject \"he\" is singular.", "1": "\"Am\" goes only with \"I\".", "3": "\"Were\" is plural and also the wrong tense."}'),
  ('ENG-0742', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Nouns ending in -s', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
Statistics ______ a compulsory subject for medical students.', '["are", "were", "is", "have been"]', 2, 'As the name of a subject, "statistics" is singular. (Used for figures, as in "The statistics show...", it is plural.)', '{"0": "As the name of a subject it takes a singular verb.", "1": "The subject is singular and the tense should be present.", "3": "The subject is singular."}'),
  ('ENG-0743', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Amounts', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
Two-thirds of the syllabus ______ been covered so far.', '["has", "have", "are", "were"]', 0, 'With fractions, the verb agrees with the noun after "of"; "syllabus" is singular.', '{"1": "\"Syllabus\" is singular.", "2": "\"Are been\" is not a verb form.", "3": "\"Were been\" is not a verb form."}'),
  ('ENG-0744', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Questions and inverted order', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
Where ______ the results of yesterday''s test?', '["is", "was", "are", "has"]', 2, 'In a question the subject comes after the verb; the subject is "the results" (plural), not "test".', '{"0": "\"Test\" is inside the \"of\" phrase; the subject is \"results\".", "1": "The subject is plural.", "3": "The subject is plural, and \"has\" does not fit before a noun phrase here."}'),
  ('ENG-0745', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Compound subjects', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 4.1', 'Choose the correct option to fill the blank(s):
Slow and steady ______ the race.', '["win", "are winning", "wins", "have won"]', 2, 'In this saying, "slow and steady" names a single idea (a steady approach), so it takes a singular verb.', '{"0": "The two adjectives together name one idea, so the verb is singular.", "1": "\"Are\" is plural and the saying states a general truth.", "3": "\"Have\" is plural."}'),
  ('ENG-0746', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Run-on sentences and comma splices', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Which sentence is a comma splice?', '["The lab was closed, we went home.", "The lab was closed, so we went home.", "Because the lab was closed, we went home.", "The lab was closed; we went home."]', 0, 'A comma splice joins two complete sentences with only a comma.', '{"1": "The conjunction ''so'' correctly joins the two clauses.", "2": "The first part is a dependent clause, so a comma is correct.", "3": "A semicolon can join two complete sentences."}'),
  ('ENG-0747', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Run-on sentences and comma splices', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Choose the best correction of the run-on sentence:
"The patient felt dizzy she sat down."', '["The patient felt dizzy, she sat down.", "The patient felt dizzy so, she sat down.", "The patient felt dizzy, so she sat down.", "The patient, felt dizzy she sat down."]', 2, 'Two independent clauses need a comma plus a conjunction (or a full stop or semicolon).', '{"0": "This is still a comma splice.", "1": "The comma belongs before ''so'', not after it.", "3": "The comma separates subject and verb and the run-on remains."}'),
  ('ENG-0748', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Dangling modifiers', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Which sentence contains a dangling modifier?', '["Walking into the ward, I noticed the smell of antiseptic.", "As I walked into the ward, the smell of antiseptic was strong.", "Walking into the ward, the smell of antiseptic was strong.", "The smell of antiseptic was strong as I walked into the ward."]', 2, 'The opening phrase ''Walking into the ward'' has no person to describe; it wrongly seems to describe ''the smell''.', '{"0": "''I'' is the one walking, so the modifier attaches correctly.", "1": "The opening is a full clause with its own subject, so nothing dangles.", "3": "The clause has its own subject, ''I''."}'),
  ('ENG-0749', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Dangling modifiers', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Choose the option that correctly completes the sentence:
Having finished the experiment, ______.', '["the students wrote their report", "the report was written by the students", "the report was easy to write", "it was time to write the report"]', 0, 'The opening phrase must describe the subject that follows; it was the students who finished the experiment.', '{"1": "This makes ''the report'' the one that finished the experiment.", "2": "The report did not finish the experiment.", "3": "''It'' cannot have finished the experiment."}'),
  ('ENG-0750', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Redundancy and wordiness', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Which sentence avoids redundancy?', '["The two rivers merged together.", "The two rivers merged together into one.", "Both of the two rivers merged together.", "The two rivers merged."]', 3, '''Merge'' already means to join together, so ''together'' or ''into one'' repeats the idea.', '{"0": "''Together'' repeats the meaning of ''merged''.", "1": "This repeats the idea twice.", "2": "''Both of the two'' and ''merged together'' are both redundant."}'),
  ('ENG-0751', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Redundancy and wordiness', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Which is the most concise correct version?
"In my personal opinion, the final outcome was a total surprise to everyone."', '["In my personal opinion, the final outcome surprised everyone.", "I think the outcome surprised everyone.", "I personally think that the final end result surprised everyone.", "In my opinion, the outcome was a surprise to each and every one."]', 1, '''My opinion'' is always personal and an outcome is always final; the concise version keeps the meaning without the padding.', '{"0": "''Personal opinion'' and ''final outcome'' are still redundant.", "2": "''Final end result'' repeats the same idea three times.", "3": "''Each and every one'' is wordier than ''everyone''."}'),
  ('ENG-0752', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Errors of function words', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Choose the correct word:
There were ______ patients in the clinic today than yesterday.', '["less", "fewer", "lesser", "little"]', 1, '''Fewer'' is used with countable plural nouns (patients); ''less'' is for uncountable nouns.', '{"0": "''Less'' is for uncountable nouns, such as less water.", "2": "''Lesser'' means smaller in importance, as in ''the lesser evil''.", "3": "''Little'' is for uncountable nouns and is not comparative."}'),
  ('ENG-0753', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Errors of function words', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Choose the correct word:
A large ______ of students applied for the scholarship.', '["amount", "deal", "quantity", "number"]', 3, '''Number'' is used with countable nouns such as students; ''amount'' is for uncountable nouns.', '{"0": "''Amount'' is for uncountable nouns, such as an amount of money.", "1": "''A great deal of'' is for uncountable nouns.", "2": "''Quantity'' is used for things measured, not for people."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0754', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Errors of function words', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Choose the correct word:
The doctor told him to ______ down on the bed for a while.', '["lie", "lay", "laid", "lain"]', 0, '''Lie'' (lie, lay, lain) means to rest flat and takes no object; ''lay'' (lay, laid, laid) means to put something down.', '{"1": "''Lay'' needs an object, as in ''lay the baby on the bed''.", "2": "''Laid'' is the past of ''lay'', and ''to'' needs the base form.", "3": "''Lain'' is a past participle and cannot follow ''to''."}'),
  ('ENG-0755', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Parallel structure', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Choose the correct sentence.', '["She likes reading, writing and to swim.", "She likes to read, writing and swimming.", "She likes reading, writing and swimming.", "She likes reading, to write and swim."]', 2, 'Items in a list should have the same grammatical form: three gerunds.', '{"0": "''To swim'' breaks the pattern of -ing forms.", "1": "''To read'' breaks the pattern.", "3": "The list mixes forms."}'),
  ('ENG-0756', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Word order', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Choose the correct order of adjectives:
She bought a ______ table.', '["lovely round wooden", "wooden round lovely", "round lovely wooden", "lovely wooden round"]', 0, 'The usual order is opinion (lovely), then shape (round), then material (wooden).', '{"1": "Material comes last, and opinion first.", "2": "Opinion comes before shape.", "3": "Shape comes before material."}'),
  ('ENG-0757', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Word order', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Choose the sentence with correct word order.', '["I don''t know what time does the clinic open.", "I don''t know what time opens the clinic.", "I don''t know what does time the clinic open.", "I don''t know what time the clinic opens."]', 3, 'In an embedded question, the subject comes before the verb and ''does'' is not used.', '{"0": "Question word order is not used inside a statement.", "1": "The subject \"the clinic\" must come before the verb.", "2": "The word order is scrambled."}'),
  ('ENG-0758', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Choosing the correct sentence', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["Scarcely had she sat down than the phone rang.", "Scarcely had she sat down when the phone rang.", "Scarcely she had sat down when the phone rang.", "Scarcely had she sit down when the phone rang."]', 1, '"Scarcely" pairs with "when" (not "than"), with inversion after it.', '{"0": "\"Than\" goes with \"No sooner\", not \"Scarcely\".", "2": "Inversion is needed after a fronted \"Scarcely\".", "3": "The past participle \"sat\" is needed."}'),
  ('ENG-0759', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Faulty comparison', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["She is the more intelligent of the two sisters.", "She is the most intelligent of the two sisters.", "She is more intelligent of the two sisters.", "She is the more intelligenter of the two sisters."]', 0, 'When comparing two, use the comparative ("more"), with "the" before it in this pattern.', '{"1": "The superlative is for three or more.", "2": "\"The\" is needed in the pattern \"the more... of the two\".", "3": "This is a double comparative."}'),
  ('ENG-0760', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spotting the error', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["Please explain me this problem.", "Please explain to me about this problem.", "Please explain this problem to me.", "Please explain this problem me."]', 2, '"Explain" does not take an indirect object without "to".', '{"0": "\"Explain me\" is a common error.", "1": "\"About\" is unnecessary after explain.", "3": "\"To\" is missing before \"me\"."}'),
  ('ENG-0761', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spotting the error', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["The reason he was late is because the bus broke down.", "The reason why he was late is because the bus broke down.", "The reason he was late is that the bus broke down.", "Because the bus broke down is the reason he was late."]', 2, '"The reason... is that" is correct; "the reason... is because" repeats the idea of cause.', '{"0": "\"Reason\" and \"because\" both express cause, so this is redundant.", "1": "This is doubly redundant.", "3": "A \"because\" clause cannot be the subject."}'),
  ('ENG-0762', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spotting the error', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Which part of the sentence contains an error?
(A) The committee discussed about (B) the proposal (C) at length (D) yesterday.', '["(A) The committee discussed about", "(B) the proposal", "(C) at length", "(D) yesterday."]', 0, '"Discuss" takes a direct object: "discussed the proposal", with no "about".', '{"1": "\"The proposal\" is correct.", "2": "\"At length\" is correct.", "3": "\"Yesterday\" is correct."}'),
  ('ENG-0763', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spotting the error', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Which part of the sentence contains an error?
(A) The teacher said that (B) each of the boys (C) have submitted (D) his assignment on time.', '["(A) The teacher said that", "(B) each of the boys", "(C) have submitted", "(D) his assignment on time."]', 2, '"Each" is singular, so the verb must be "has submitted".', '{"0": "\"The teacher said that\" is correct.", "1": "\"Each of the boys\" is a correct subject.", "3": "\"His\" correctly agrees with the singular \"each\"."}'),
  ('ENG-0764', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spotting the error', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Which part of the sentence contains an error?
(A) No sooner did the doctor arrive (B) when the patient (C) began to feel (D) better.', '["(A) No sooner did the doctor arrive", "(B) when the patient", "(C) began to feel", "(D) better."]', 1, '"No sooner" pairs with "than", not "when": "No sooner did the doctor arrive than the patient began to feel better."', '{"0": "\"No sooner did the doctor arrive\" is correct inversion.", "2": "\"Began to feel\" is correct.", "3": "\"Better\" is correct."}'),
  ('ENG-0765', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Double negatives', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["Hardly no one came to the meeting.", "Hardly anyone came to the meeting.", "Hardly nobody came to the meeting.", "Not hardly anyone came to the meeting."]', 1, '"Hardly" is already negative, so it is used with "anyone", not "no one".', '{"0": "This is a double negative.", "2": "This is a double negative.", "3": "This is a double negative."}'),
  ('ENG-0766', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spotting the error', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["She is married with a doctor.", "She is married by a doctor.", "She is married from a doctor.", "She is married to a doctor."]', 3, 'The fixed form is "married to".', '{"0": "\"Married with\" is a common error.", "1": "\"By\" would mean the doctor performed the ceremony.", "2": "\"From\" is not used."}'),
  ('ENG-0767', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spotting the error', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["The patient is too weak to walk.", "The patient is so weak to walk.", "The patient is too weak that he cannot walk.", "The patient is very weak to walk."]', 0, 'The pattern is "too + adjective + to-infinitive".', '{"1": "\"So\" pairs with \"that\" (so weak that he cannot walk), not with \"to\".", "2": "\"Too\" is not followed by a \"that\" clause.", "3": "\"Very\" does not combine with a to-infinitive of result."}'),
  ('ENG-0768', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Errors of function words', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["She gave me some useful advices.", "She gave me a useful advice.", "She gave me an useful advice.", "She gave me some useful advice."]', 3, '"Advice" is uncountable: it has no plural and does not take "a" or "an".', '{"0": "\"Advice\" has no plural form.", "1": "An uncountable noun does not take \"a\"; say \"a piece of advice\".", "2": "\"Advice\" takes no article here, and \"useful\" would take \"a\", not \"an\"."}'),
  ('ENG-0769', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spelling', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Which word is spelled correctly?', '["millenium", "millennium", "milennium", "milenium"]', 1, 'It has a double "l" and a double "n": m-i-l-l-e-n-n-i-u-m.', '{"0": "One \"n\" is missing.", "2": "One \"l\" is missing.", "3": "One \"l\" and one \"n\" are missing."}'),
  ('ENG-0770', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Choosing the correct sentence', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1, 3.2, 3.4; NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmark 5.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["I have never seen such large crowd.", "I have never seen such a large crowd.", "I have never seen so large crowd.", "I have never seen a such large crowd."]', 1, 'The pattern is "such a + adjective + singular noun".', '{"0": "The article \"a\" is needed before a singular countable noun.", "2": "\"So\" is followed directly by an adjective, not \"adjective + noun\" without \"a\".", "3": "\"A\" comes after \"such\"."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT INTO question_counters (prefix, n) VALUES ('ENG', 770) ON CONFLICT (prefix) DO UPDATE SET n = max(n, 770);

-- English in the AKU bank (every question) and the NUMS bank (all but the literary ones), under the same chapter.
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO bank_questions (bank, question_id, subject, chapter)
SELECT 'aku', q.id, 'English', q.chapter FROM questions q
WHERE q.id BETWEEN 'ENG-0474' AND 'ENG-0770' AND q.source LIKE 'Markhor Prep original question.%';
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO bank_questions (bank, question_id, subject, chapter)
SELECT 'nums', q.id, 'English', q.chapter FROM questions q
WHERE q.id BETWEEN 'ENG-0474' AND 'ENG-0770' AND q.source LIKE 'Markhor Prep original question.%'
  AND q.id NOT IN ('ENG-0504', 'ENG-0505', 'ENG-0506', 'ENG-0507', 'ENG-0509', 'ENG-0526', 'ENG-0527', 'ENG-0528', 'ENG-0529', 'ENG-0530', 'ENG-0531', 'ENG-0550', 'ENG-0551', 'ENG-0552', 'ENG-0553', 'ENG-0554', 'ENG-0555');
